select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"74906c19-2a9e-4d2a-a4bd-7b5b03285dfe","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Open Day at Brindle","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Brindle civic clock workshop","crime":"The brass counterweight is missing from inside the demonstration clock, which stopped at twenty to eleven on open day with the wax seal across its front still whole.","difficulty":"easy","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"dd0931ab8dfc3a1ad38ce182a4219f18dfa633d9747e02fd7f85f7f58ab73b6d","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Open day at the Brindle workshop is the one morning a year the trust brings its guests in, and the demonstration clock is what they come to see. It stopped at twenty to eleven, while the steward had the tour group out in the yard looking at the bell frame. Nobody was hurt. When Mara Vell reset the clock afterwards the brass counterweight, four and a half kilos of it, was gone from inside the case, and the wax seal across the display front was unbroken. Mara has kept that clock for eleven years and was the member of staff left in the room during the break. The trust meets next week on a quote to move the clock to a contractor''s premises, and her bench rent is in arrears. A metal merchant made his usual Saturday collection from the lane before eleven and left a slip in the gate box. You have the key ledger, the slip and the steward''s account, and the trust would like an answer before it meets.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Mara Vell","ageRange":"late forties","role":"clock restorer","avatarDescription":"Canvas apron over a wool jumper, cuffs rolled back off hands that have been in oil, a loupe on a cord round her neck.","speechStyle":"Workshop words and few of them. Explains the clock more readily than herself.","attitude":"Apologises for the state of the bench before she has sat down, and keeps glancing across at the stopped clock as if it might start.","relationship":"Rents the bench from the trust; the clock came to the workshop the same year she did, and it is the reason anyone walks through the door."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"Mara opened the rear maintenance hatch with her triangular service key while the tour was out, lifted out the counterweight, wrapped it in her padded tool roll and handed it to the metal merchant in the lane before eleven.","motive":"The trust meets next week on a contractor''s quote to move the clock out of her workshop, and the clock is the only reason her bench earns anything. The counterweight is a bespoke casting that the clock cannot run without, and under the trust''s own rules a missing part is commissioned from the keeper at her rates. A clock that cannot run cannot be moved on the contractor''s schedule, the refit would take months of paid work, and the fee would clear her arrears. The merchant''s van in the lane was simply the quickest way to get the weight off the premises before anyone searched.","opportunity":"She was alone with the clock from half past ten until ten to eleven while the steward had the tour in the yard.","means":"The triangular service key opens the rear hatch, which sits outside the wax seal on the front display.","realAlibi":"I was at my bench by the clock the whole time the tour was out, from half past ten till ten to eleven.","explanation":"The seal rules out the front and nothing else; the rear hatch opens with the one triangular key, issued to Mara. The key board log has that key out at ten thirty-four and back after eleven under her initials, against her claim that it stayed on its hook. The merchant''s seller counterfoil for the brass, with her initials on the name line, was folded in her tool roll. The trust minutes carry the quote to move the clock, her arrears, and the rule that a missing part is commissioned from the keeper, which is the money in it. The yard volunteer''s scrap bin held iron fittings and was weighed as such. The father fetched a cap and was in the room under a minute with the steward at the door.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-14T10:00:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T10:28:00Z","location":"clock bench","description":"Mara readies the demonstration clock for the open day tour.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-14T10:30:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T10:50:00Z","location":"clock bench","description":"Mara stays at the bench while the steward has the tour group out in the yard.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-14T10:38:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T10:42:00Z","location":"clock bench","description":"Mara opens the rear hatch with the triangular key, lifts out the counterweight and wraps it in her tool roll.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-14T10:55:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T11:03:00Z","location":"workshop lane","description":"Mara hands the roll to the metal merchant in the lane, takes the counterfoil and goes back in to return the key.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-14T11:30:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T11:31:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Mara sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"clock bench","from":"2026-03-14T09:00:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T13:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"suspect","location":"workshop lane","from":"2026-03-14T09:00:00Z","until":"2026-03-14T13:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The rear maintenance hatch opens with the triangular service key and leaves the front seal untouched.","disclosurePhrases":["The rear hatch takes the triangular key.","There''s a hatch round the back that my key opens.","The front seal stays put when the back hatch comes off.","You can get at the works from the back hatch."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I had the triangular key off the board during the tour break.","disclosurePhrases":["I had the key out during the break.","The triangular key was off its hook this morning.","The key was at my bench while the tour was out.","I took the key down before the break."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"Once the tour was back in I went out to the lane with my padded tool roll, just before eleven.","disclosurePhrases":["I took my tool roll out to the lane.","I went out to the lane before eleven.","My tool roll went out to the van with me.","I walked the tool roll out to the lane myself."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"The trust wants to move the clock to a contractor, my rent is three months behind, and a missing part has to be commissioned from me.","disclosurePhrases":["The trust wants the clock moved out.","We''re three months behind on the rent.","A missing part gets commissioned from the keeper.","The clock is the only reason anyone comes in."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4"],"doesNotKnow":["What the steward or the merchant have told anyone.","What the yard volunteer put in the scrap bin."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I had the triangular key off the board during the tour break.","permittedDeception":"I may insist the triangular key stayed on its hook all morning and that I had no call to touch the hatch, until the key board log is put to me."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Merchant''s slip left at the gate","description":"Gate box: the metal merchant''s lane slip, left for the trust as the lane rules require: Saturday, \"brass, four and a half kilos, cash paid\", weighed on the van scale, seller''s line blank. The merchant''s slips come in two halves; the gate half records the load, and the seller keeps the half with the name on it.","category":"record","source":"Gate box","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Brass of the right weight left by the lane that morning. The gate half does not say from whom, and the seller''s half is somewhere with a name on it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Key ledger entry for the service key","description":"Workshop key ledger: triangular service key, one of one, issued to M. Vell on her first day; kept on the key board when not in use; the board hangs in the workshop corridor, in reach of anyone passing, and the ledger''s \"copies made\" column reads none.","category":"record","source":"Workshop key ledger","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Mara is the only person issued the key that opens the rear hatch, and the board it hangs on is in a public corridor.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Steward on the tour break","description":"Steward''s statement: the group was in the yard from half past ten until ten to eleven; Mara was at her bench when they went out, with the clock ticking, and at it again when they came back in, with the clock stopped; at about a quarter to eleven the yard volunteer wheeled the scrap bin through the workshop to the lane, and the merchant''s van was already parked there with its tailgate down.","category":"testimony","source":"Steward''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Mara at both ends of the break and unseen in between, and the clock stopped while she was. One other person crossed the room, pushing a bin to a scrap merchant whose van was waiting.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Rear hatch screws","description":"Scene inspection: bright scratches round the four rear hatch screws and a pinch of clean brass dust on the floor beneath the case.","category":"physical","source":"Scene inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The hatch has been off recently. Mara admits it exists and that her key opens it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Key board log for the morning","description":"Key board log, Saturday: triangular key out 10:34, back 11:02, initials M.V. in both columns.","category":"record","source":"Workshop key ledger","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Contradicts any claim that the key stayed on its hook, and brackets the stopped clock.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Merchant''s counterfoil in the tool roll","description":"Tool roll search: the seller''s half of the merchant''s Saturday brass slip, name line \"M.V., restorer\", folded into the padded pocket.","category":"physical","source":"Tool roll search","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The blank gate slip has its other half, and it was in her roll with her initials on it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Trust minutes for March","description":"Trust minutes, March: a contractor''s quote to move the demonstration clock to its own premises after open day, tabled for the lease review on the twentieth; bench rent for M. Vell three months in arrears at £380 a month; standing rule that any part the clock needs is commissioned from the keeper at her rates before the clock travels, the last such commission, a replacement escapement in 2019, billed at £2,900.","category":"record","source":"Trust minutes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The clock is about to leave her workshop, with £1,140 of arrears on the same page. A clock missing a part only she can make stays put, and the last refit paid nearly three thousand.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Steward on the boy''s father","description":"Steward''s statement: during the break the father of a boy Mara had told off went back into the workshop for the boy''s cap and came straight out again.","category":"red_herring","source":"Steward''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He was in and out in under a minute with the steward watching from the door, and the cap was on the coat hook by the entrance.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Is the front the only way into that clock?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Where was the triangular key this morning?","category":"access","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"How''s the workshop doing for money?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"What happens to the clock after the lease review?","category":"motive","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Who had that hatch off today?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-6","text":"What went out to the lane in your tool roll?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Where did you go once the tour came back in?","category":"alibi","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"Brass of exactly the weight''s mass went to the merchant that morning for cash."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The board log has her drawing the one key that opens the hatch, minutes before the clock stopped, against her own account."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The seller''s half of the merchant''s slip carries her initials, and it was in her roll."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-2","why":"The father fetched a cap from the hook by the door in under a minute, watched by the steward."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["How does anyone get inside the case with the seal still on?","How would anyone get at the works without breaking that seal?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The seal only covers the front; the rear hatch and her key are the route."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Was the triangular key off its hook this morning?","Did you have the key out during the break?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She denies it until the board log is put to her, then admits the key was off the board."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Where did your tool roll go after the break?","What went out to the lane with you?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The roll went to the merchant, and the counterfoil in it carries her initials."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='dd0931ab8dfc3a1ad38ce182a4219f18dfa633d9747e02fd7f85f7f58ab73b6d');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"7738fe0d-1fa8-4996-a246-726a6e517031","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Six Trays, Then Five","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Hartwell botanical conservatory","crime":"One of six trays of Hartwell violet seedlings is gone from the propagation shelf, label and all, between the six o''clock check and seven.","difficulty":"medium","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"d412d3d5c5837025d9094d715771f5c527fd177f2d1e7f94b772b8450957441c","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Six trays of the Hartwell violet were on the propagation shelf at six on Tuesday evening, when the curator ticked the check. At seven there were five. The glasshouse was closed to the public for the night. Four years of work went into that seedling line and collectors have asked for it for most of them, so the conservatory wants to know who lifted a tray and how it left the building. Danny Fen was on the watering round, alone in the glasshouse for the hour, and his initials are on the violet row of the watering sheet with no time beside them. The loading bay door opened twice in the window on a push-bar that logs the time and nothing else. A volunteer heard singing at the far end and took it for a radio. Danny is six weeks into a job he moved towns for. Decide whether he took the tray.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Danny Fen","ageRange":"early twenties","role":"night gardener","avatarDescription":"Damp waxed jacket, cap held in both hands, a smear of compost on one cheekbone.","speechStyle":"Talks fast and a lot, and circles back to add detail nobody asked for.","attitude":"Expansive and a bit cocky, treats the whole thing as a story he will be telling his sister later.","relationship":"Six weeks into a probationary post the curator signs off at the end of the month; moved towns for it and still owes his sister the deposit on his room."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"curator","actualEvent":"The curator, Rosalind Mayhew, lifted the Hartwell violet tray onto the outbound nursery trolley at about twenty past six, rewrote its destination tag in the green ink she keeps for dispatch, pushed the trolley through the bay door to stage it at twenty-five past, and signed it out to the evening courier at a quarter to seven.","motive":"The curator had privately promised the tray to a collector in exchange for a specimen the conservatory could not afford.","opportunity":"The curator had the glasshouse and the loading bay to herself between the evening check and the courier.","means":"A rewritten destination tag on the courier trolley sent the tray out with seven legitimate ones.","realAlibi":"I was watering the orchid bench at the far end of the glasshouse from about ten past six until seven, and between runs I was singing to my phone, sort of practising.","explanation":"Danny initials every row on the sheet before the round and fills in times as he goes; he never reached the violets because the orchids took the hour. His wand had been on the shelf hook since four and the shelf was ticked full at six. His phone recording runs through the whole half hour with the orchid misters and the glasshouse clock behind him. He saw the curator stop the trolley at the violet shelf. The bay tally has the curator staging the trolley at twenty-five past and signing it to the courier at a quarter to, as she does on every dispatch night, and the courier''s eighth tray carries the violet label with its tag rewritten to a private address in the green dispatch ink from her desk; her call to the trustees was on the cordless handset, which went to the bay with her. The grower who asked about the shelf was in the car park waiting for a lift and never came back in.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-03T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T18:10:00Z","location":"potting room","description":"Danny fills the cans, initials every row on the watering sheet and checks the misting rota.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-03T18:10:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T19:00:00Z","location":"orchid bench","description":"Danny waters the orchid bench at the far end of the glasshouse, recording himself singing between runs.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"curator","at":"2026-02-03T18:18:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T18:24:00Z","location":"violet shelf","description":"The curator lifts the Hartwell violet tray onto the nursery trolley and rewrites its destination tag in green ink.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"curator","at":"2026-02-03T18:25:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T18:27:00Z","location":"loading bay","description":"The curator pushes the trolley through the bay door and leaves it staged for the courier.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"curator","at":"2026-02-03T18:40:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T18:46:00Z","location":"loading bay","description":"The curator signs the trolley out to the evening courier.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-6","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-03T19:20:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T19:21:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Danny sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"orchid bench","from":"2026-02-03T17:30:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T21:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"curator","location":"violet shelf","from":"2026-02-03T08:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T21:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"curator","location":"loading bay","from":"2026-02-03T08:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-03T21:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"I left my watering wand on the shelf hook after the afternoon misting, around four.","disclosurePhrases":["I left the wand on the hook around four.","That wand''s been there since the afternoon misting.","I put the wand down there this afternoon.","The wand was there hours before any of this."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I initial every row on the watering sheet before the round and fill in the times as I go, and the orchids took the whole hour.","disclosurePhrases":["I initial the whole sheet before I start.","The times go in as I get to each shelf.","The orchids took me the whole hour.","I was at the orchid bench from ten past six."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The curator wheeled the nursery trolley down the centre aisle, which runs straight from the orchid bench to the violet shelf, and stopped at the shelf at about twenty past six.","disclosurePhrases":["The curator came through with the trolley.","I saw the curator stop at the violet shelf.","The trolley went past me around twenty past six.","The curator was at that shelf with the trolley."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I was recording myself singing for a community choir audition between watering runs.","disclosurePhrases":["I was recording myself singing for an audition.","That singing was me, for the choir audition.","I''ve got a choir audition next week.","The phone was recording me singing scales."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"Collectors have been asking for Hartwell violets for years and a single tray would fetch a lot from one of them.","disclosurePhrases":["Collectors have been after those violets for years.","A tray like that would fetch real money from a collector.","There''s a waiting list of collectors for the violets.","People would pay a lot for one tray."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["Where the tray went after the trolley left.","Why the curator stopped at the shelf.","What the courier signed for."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-4","realFact":"I was recording myself singing for a community choir audition between watering runs.","permittedDeception":"I may call the singing the radio or equipment checks until the detective asks directly about the recording on my phone."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Empty square on the violet shelf","description":"Scene inspection: one labelled tray space on the propagation shelf is bare, the damp outline still showing, with the five other Hartwell trays untouched either side; a watering wand tagged with Danny''s locker number lies under the shelf.","category":"physical","source":"Scene inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"One tray lifted cleanly by someone who knew which, and Danny''s tool at the scene with no time on it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Watering sheet for the evening round","description":"Watering sheet, Tuesday: every row initialled D.F.; times filled in for the fern house and the orchid bench; the violet shelf row carries his initials and a blank time.","category":"record","source":"Watering sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"His initials sit against the violet shelf with no time. Either he was there and left it blank, or he initialled it before he went anywhere.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Loading bay push-bar log","description":"Bay door log: push-bar openings at 18:25 and 18:45; the bar records the time and nothing else. The office phone log has the curator on a call to the trustees from 18:05 to 18:50.","category":"record","source":"Bay door log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Something could have left by the bay twice in the window. The log cannot say who or what, and the curator was on the phone through both.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Volunteer on the singing","description":"Volunteer''s statement: locking the potting room at about half past six, she heard someone singing at the far end of the glasshouse and took it for a radio.","category":"testimony","source":"Volunteer''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Someone was at the far end at half past six, or a radio was. Which, and for how long, needs a record.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Misting rota and the six o''clock tick","description":"Misting rota: afternoon misting signed off at four by Danny, wand returned to the shelf hook; shelf ticked \"six trays\" at six by the curator.","category":"record","source":"Misting rota","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The wand was on that hook two hours before the shelf was last seen full.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Recording on Danny''s phone","description":"Phone recording, 18:12 to 18:43: scales and a ballad, orchid misters hissing underneath, the glasshouse clock striking the half hour.","category":"record","source":"Phone recording","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Continuous sound from the orchid end through the whole window, with the room''s own noises behind it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Eighth tray on the courier van","description":"Courier van check: the bay tally has the nursery trolley staged at 18:25 and signed to the courier at 18:45 by R. Mayhew, curator, as on every dispatch night; the trolley carries eight trays against a dispatch entry of seven; the eighth bears the Hartwell violet label, destination rewritten over the original in the green dispatch ink that lives in the curator''s desk tidy, to a private address the courier''s manifest has in the same hand as the dispatch entries; the office handset is cordless, and the courier heard her on it at the bay.","category":"physical","source":"Courier van check","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The missing tray went out on the trolley the curator stages every night, with a tag rewritten in her ink to a collector''s address, and the phone call travelled with her.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Volunteer on the visiting grower","description":"Volunteer''s statement: a visiting grower asked twice during the afternoon which shelf held the Hartwell violets, photographed the labels, and was still in the car park at half past six.","category":"red_herring","source":"Volunteer''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The grower was waiting for a lift, in sight of the desk, and never came back inside after four.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"How did your wand end up under the violet shelf?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Were you at the violet shelf tonight?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"Who''d pay for a tray of those violets?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Who was singing at the far end at half past six?","category":"evidence","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Did anyone come through while you were at the far end?","category":"timeline","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Who could move a whole tray without anyone noticing?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-5","why":"Someone was at the far end at half past six, and the recording shows it was Danny, for the whole window."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The recording keeps Danny at the orchid end, with the room''s own sounds behind him, through the whole window."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The violet tray is on the trolley the curator staged, one tray over the dispatch entry, with its tag rewritten in her ink to a private address."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-4","why":"The grower was in the car park in sight of the desk and never came back inside."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Is that your wand under the shelf?","When did you last use the wand?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The wand went on the hook at four and predates the theft."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Where were you at twenty past six?","Why is the time blank on the violet row?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He initials the sheet before the round; the orchids took the hour."},{"questionId":"question-4","alternativePhrasings":["What was the singing the volunteer heard?","What''s on your phone from tonight?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"He admits the audition recording, which unlocks a continuous sound record."},{"questionId":"question-5","alternativePhrasings":["Did anyone pass you while you were watering?","Who else was in the glasshouse?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"He saw the curator stop the trolley at the violet shelf."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='d412d3d5c5837025d9094d715771f5c527fd177f2d1e7f94b772b8450957441c');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"84359e4d-471c-4799-a472-05ba3565ce8c","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Reversed Festival Arrows","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Ternbridge lantern festival","crime":"Two junction signs on the paid lantern route were deliberately reversed before the gates opened, sending the morning crowd down Quay Lane and away from the Mill Yard stalls.","difficulty":"hard","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"1aba6b114608e92ad17434788b677fbdb16bafee44d34ec851a7abd8b90f6a24","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"By half past eight on the first paying morning of the Ternbridge lantern festival, the Mill Yard stallholders were at the office door. The two arrows at the west junction pointed the wrong way, and the first few hundred visitors through the gate had been walked down Quay Lane instead. Both boards were correct when the committee signed them off in the route book on Friday evening. Nessa Quill coordinates the route and holds the office keys. Her family have traded on Quay Lane since her father''s time, and last year the Mill Yard chair accused her in a public meeting of fixing the pitch draw against him, which is why she ran this year''s draw with a witness and the Mill Yard stalls drew the gate. A signage contractor had a crew at that junction from seven on a scheduled collection, and a Quay Lane stallholder heard someone at the boards in the dark before that. Nessa is in the office with the route book and the gate count.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Nessa Quill","ageRange":"mid thirties","role":"route coordinator","avatarDescription":"A high-visibility tabard over a smart coat, and a lanyard so heavy with keys that it makes a sound when she turns.","speechStyle":"Procedural. Quotes the schedule and gives times to the minute.","attitude":"Composed and courteous, with one eye on the radio clipped to her tabard.","relationship":"Three seasons on the route; fell out in public with the Mill Yard stallholders'' chair over last year''s pitch draw, and the two have not spoken since. Her brother has the family pie stall on Quay Lane."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"Nessa printed a mirrored arrow sheet on the office printer just after six, pasted the overlays onto both west junction boards at twenty to seven, radioed an all clear, and was back in the office before the gates.","motive":"Her brother Davey runs the family pie stall, pitch seven on Quay Lane, and drew the far end of the route in the pitch draw she ran with a witness to answer last year''s accusation; she wanted the first-morning crowd walked past him anyway.","opportunity":"She had the route to herself between the stallholders'' setup at half past six and the contractor''s arrival at seven.","means":"A mirrored print setting and removable paste put a wrong arrow over each painted board without marking it.","realAlibi":"I was in the festival office from half past seven doing the gate count, and I was there when the complaint came in at half past eight.","explanation":"The arrows are paper overlays, which is a print job, and the printer log has a mirrored arrow sheet under Nessa''s user at seven minutes past six, against her claim that she printed only labels. The steward''s radio log puts her at the west junction at eighteen minutes to seven, which is when the Quay Lane stallholder heard the scraping, and before the contractor''s crew signed in at seven. The gate tally shows Quay Lane gained, and the licence register shows that pitch seven is her brother''s. The contractor''s docket covers four empty frames stacked behind the boards; the boards were already pasted when the crew arrived.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-09T06:00:00Z","until":"2026-05-09T06:15:00Z","location":"festival office","description":"Nessa prints the mirrored arrow sheet on the office printer.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-09T06:35:00Z","until":"2026-05-09T06:50:00Z","location":"west junction","description":"Nessa pastes the overlays onto both junction boards and radios an all clear.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-09T07:30:00Z","until":"2026-05-09T09:00:00Z","location":"festival office","description":"Nessa runs the gate count; the Mill Yard complaint arrives at half past eight.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-09T09:30:00Z","until":"2026-05-09T09:31:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Nessa sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"festival office","from":"2026-05-09T05:30:00Z","until":"2026-05-09T12:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"suspect","location":"west junction","from":"2026-05-09T05:30:00Z","until":"2026-05-09T12:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The arrows are paper overlays pasted over the painted boards.","disclosurePhrases":["They''re paper overlays stuck on the boards.","Someone pasted paper over the painted arrows.","You can peel those arrows off with a thumbnail.","The boards themselves are still painted right."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I used the office printer just after six this morning.","disclosurePhrases":["I was on the office printer just after six.","I did run a job through the printer early.","The early print job was mine.","I printed something before the stalls set up."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"I walked the route past the west junction at about twenty to seven, before the gates opened.","disclosurePhrases":["I was at the west junction before the gates opened.","I walked the route around twenty to seven.","I checked the junction boards myself early on.","I was out on the route before seven."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"Pitch seven on Quay Lane is the family pie stall, and my brother Davey runs it.","disclosurePhrases":["Pitch seven is my brother''s.","My brother runs the pie stall on Quay Lane.","Davey on Quay Lane is my brother.","The Quay Lane pie stall is family."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"I ran the pitch draw this year with a witness, and the Mill Yard stalls drew the junction nearest the gate.","disclosurePhrases":["I ran the pitch draw this year.","Mill Yard won the gate pitch in my draw.","The draw gave Mill Yard the better junction.","Quay Lane drew the far end of the route."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["What the stallholders have told the office.","Whether the steward kept the radio log.","What the contractor''s crew saw."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I used the office printer just after six this morning.","permittedDeception":"I may insist the only thing I have printed this week was the lantern labels, on Thursday, until the printer job log is put to me."},{"factId":"fact-4","realFact":"Pitch seven on Quay Lane is the family pie stall, and my brother Davey runs it.","permittedDeception":"I may describe the pitch seven stallholder as someone I know from committee meetings until asked straight whether we are related."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Paste along the board edges","description":"Route steward''s walk: both west junction boards carry a line of fresh paste along the top edge and a faint paper lip at one corner; the route boards are painted, and nothing is pasted to them in the normal way of things.","category":"physical","source":"Route steward''s walk","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The boards were altered with something stuck on, recently, rather than turned or repainted.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Gate and stall tallies at nine","description":"Festival office: 906 passes through the gate by 09:00; stall tallies at 09:00, Mill Yard 41, Quay Lane 212.","category":"record","source":"Festival office","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Quay Lane gained the crowd. Who on Quay Lane gained most is a separate question.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Mill Yard stallholder on the arrows","description":"Stallholder''s statement: a Mill Yard stallholder set up at half past six with both arrows pointing her way.","category":"testimony","source":"Stallholder''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Narrows the change to between half past six and the gates at eight.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Quay Lane stallholder on the noise","description":"Stallholder''s statement: a Quay Lane stallholder unloading in the dark at about twenty to seven heard scraping and muttering from the west junction boards and took it for the contractor''s crew.","category":"testimony","source":"Stallholder''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Someone was at the boards at twenty to seven. The stallholder assumed who; nobody saw.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Peeled corner on the west board","description":"Route steward''s walk: lifting the paper lip on the west board shows the painted arrow beneath pointing the approved way.","category":"physical","source":"Route steward''s walk","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Overlays, so the arrows were printed and placed, which needs a printer and a morning.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Printer job log","description":"Festival office printer log: 06:07, \"junction arrows mirror\", 2 pages, user Quill; the job before it is \"lantern labels\", Thursday 16:20, user Quill.","category":"record","source":"Festival office","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Against her account of printing only labels. A mirrored arrow job is the overlay.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Radio log entry from the west junction","description":"Steward''s radio log: 06:42, Quill from the west junction, \"boards fine, all clear\"; contractor''s crew signs in at the gate at 07:00.","category":"testimony","source":"Steward''s radio log","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Her own voice puts her at the boards at the minute the stallholder heard scraping, twenty minutes before the crew arrived.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Stall licence for pitch seven","description":"Stall licence register: Quay Lane pitch seven, \"Quill''s Pies\", licensee D. Quill, pitch assigned by the coordinator''s draw on the first of April.","category":"record","source":"Stall licence register","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The stall that gained shares her surname and lost the draw she ran.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Quay Lane stallholder on the contractor","description":"Stallholder''s statement: from seven a Quay Lane stallholder watched the signage contractor''s two crew at the west junction with a van, handling the frames stacked behind the boards.","category":"red_herring","source":"Stallholder''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The crew signed in at the gate at seven for four empty frames, after the boards were already pasted, and their docket says so.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"What''s the paste along the top of the junction boards for?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Who gains if the crowd goes down Quay Lane instead of Mill Yard?","category":"motive","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"How did the pitches get decided this year?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Where were you from six this morning until the gates opened?","category":"alibi","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"What came off the office printer this morning?","category":"access","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Where did that print job go once it was in your hands?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"The tally shows which lane gained the crowd, which is where the motive lives."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"A mirrored arrow job under her user at six, against her claim of printing only labels."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"Her own radio call puts her at the boards at the minute of the scraping, before the crew arrived."},{"evidenceId":"record-4","why":"The stall that gained is her brother''s and lost the draw she ran."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The crew signed in at seven for four empty frames, on a docket; the boards were pasted twenty minutes earlier."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["How do two arrows end up backwards without a mark on the boards?","Were the boards turned round?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"Overlays mean a print job, which is a record."},{"questionId":"question-5","alternativePhrasings":["Did you print anything this morning?","Would the printer log show your jobs?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She claims labels only, then concedes an early job when the log is put to her."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Were you on the route before the gates?","Who put the sheet on the boards?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"She was at the west junction at twenty to seven, inside the window."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Who runs the pie stall on Quay Lane?","Any family trading at the festival?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"The gaining stall is her brother''s."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='1aba6b114608e92ad17434788b677fbdb16bafee44d34ec851a7abd8b90f6a24');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"7f6ac8c5-4f07-4aaf-adf8-c558f232678f","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Wednesday Count","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Wrenfold community theatre","crime":"The sealed envelope of the weekend''s ticket takings was gone from the stage office cash box by a quarter to nine, during the Wednesday rehearsal.","difficulty":"easy","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"2e17a8ab633949a5a5a324ad7434cd55a773eae4f331d52f00b577dd6ce9b923","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Wrenfold Players count the weekend''s takings on the Wednesday after, because that is the rehearsal night when the treasurer and the director are both in the building to sign the count. Saturday''s takings were counted and sealed at eight and locked in the stage office cash box. At a quarter to nine the envelope was gone. The drawer had been shut again and the lock showed no marks. The stage manager went down the corridor for the director''s notes at ten past eight and heard Tavi Reed''s voice coming from inside the office. Tavi was on sound, which means the booth upstairs, and her signed cue sheet for the second act has three cues ticked in a different pen at about that moment. A props crate, the costume hamper and the bar float tin went out through the loading bay between eight and nine, and she deleted a file from the booth computer the moment rehearsal ended. Ask her what you need to, then say whether she took the envelope.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Tavi Reed","ageRange":"nineteen","role":"sound operator","avatarDescription":"Oversized black crew hoodie with the sleeves shoved up, headphones round her neck.","speechStyle":"Fast run-on sentences with \"like\" and \"honestly\" scattered through them, swinging between indignant and sorry.","attitude":"Talkative, quick to apologise, talks straight through any pause.","relationship":"Volunteer on sound for two seasons; has twice asked the committee for a paid technician''s post and twice been told the takings could not fund it."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"treasurer","actualEvent":"The treasurer took the sealed envelope from the cash box at about a quarter past eight while fetching the costume hamper from the office corridor, tucked it under the hamper''s lid strap, and wheeled the hamper out through the loading bay to a car.","motive":"The treasurer needed cash to cover a shortfall in the club''s separate bar account before the annual audit.","opportunity":"The treasurer held an office key and had a reason to be in the corridor with the hamper during the second act run.","means":"A legitimate office key and the costume hamper, signed out for dry cleaning, carried the envelope out unseen.","realAlibi":"I was in the sound booth running the second act cues from just after eight until twenty to nine, and I sang a bit into the talkback while they reset the stage.","explanation":"The office monitor is fed from the booth talkback, so Tavi''s voice in the office was her voice in the booth. The cue computer logged eighteen presses from the desk through the window, including the three ticked in black while her red pen was on the floor under the rack. The loading bay camera shows the treasurer wheeling the hamper out with a white envelope under the lid strap, while the stalls board still had the treasurer marked in. The booth stairs come down beside the stage manager''s desk, and she saw nobody come down them before or after her minute in the corridor. The deleted file was her singing the lead''s part, recovered from the recycle bin.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-22T19:30:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T20:00:00Z","location":"sound booth","description":"Tavi sets up the desk and checks levels for the second act run.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-22T20:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T20:40:00Z","location":"sound booth","description":"Tavi runs the second act cues, drops her red pen under the rack at cue nine and finds it again at cue twelve, and sings the lead''s part into the talkback during the reset.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"treasurer","at":"2026-04-22T20:12:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T20:18:00Z","location":"stage office","description":"The treasurer takes the sealed envelope from the cash box and tucks it under the hamper''s lid strap.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"treasurer","at":"2026-04-22T20:24:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T20:28:00Z","location":"loading bay","description":"The treasurer wheels the hamper out to the car park.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-22T21:45:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T21:46:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Tavi sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"sound booth","from":"2026-04-22T19:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T22:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"treasurer","location":"stage office","from":"2026-04-22T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T22:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"treasurer","location":"loading bay","from":"2026-04-22T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-22T22:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The office monitor speaker is wired to the booth talkback mic, and the switch was left live.","disclosurePhrases":["The office speaker''s on the booth talkback.","That monitor picks up my talkback mic.","The talkback switch was left live all night.","Whatever I speak into the booth mic comes out in the office."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I was in the booth pressing cues for the second act run from just after eight until twenty to nine, and my red pen rolled under the rack at cue nine until I found it at twelve.","disclosurePhrases":["I was in the booth running cues the whole act.","I pressed every cue in that act from the booth.","The red pen rolled under the rack at cue nine.","I was at the desk from eight till twenty to nine."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The booth stairs come down beside the stage manager''s desk, and I would have had to pass her desk to get anywhere near the office.","disclosurePhrases":["The booth stairs come down by the stage manager.","Anyone leaving the booth passes her desk.","I would have walked right past her desk.","The stage manager sits at the foot of the booth stairs."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I was singing the lead''s part into the talkback while the stage was being reset.","disclosurePhrases":["I was singing the lead''s part.","I''d been learning the lead''s songs.","That file was me singing, honestly.","I want to audition for the lead."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The treasurer and the director count the takings and hold the only two office keys; I have a booth key.","disclosurePhrases":["The treasurer and the director hold the office keys.","Only two people have keys to that office.","The takings get counted by the treasurer and the director.","I''ve got a booth key and that''s it."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["Who took the hamper out, or what was under its lid.","Anything about the club''s accounts.","Who signed the loading bay book."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-4","realFact":"I was singing the lead''s part into the talkback while the stage was being reset.","permittedDeception":"I may say the file I deleted was a test tone and that the voice in the office was me calling a cue, until the detective presses twice."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Stage office drawer and lock","description":"Scene inspection: the cash box drawer in the stage office is shut and its lock carries no marks; the envelope''s slot inside is empty.","category":"physical","source":"Scene inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Whoever opened it had a key or found it open. Nothing was forced.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Stage manager on the voice in the office","description":"Stage manager''s statement: at about ten past eight, collecting the director''s notes from the corridor, the stage manager heard Tavi''s voice coming from inside the office.","category":"testimony","source":"Stage manager''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Her voice was in the office. Where the rest of her was is a different question.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Tavi''s cue sheet for the second act","description":"Cue sheet, signed T. Reed: cues one to eighteen ticked; cues nine, ten and eleven, which fall around ten past eight, are ticked in black where the rest are in red.","category":"record","source":"Cue sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Three cues in a different pen at the time that matters. Either she changed pens, or someone else ticked them.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Loading bay book, eight to nine","description":"Loading bay book: props crate out at ten past eight, costume hamper at twenty-five past, bar float tin at twenty to nine; all three sets of initials smudged by rain. The stalls sign-in board by the auditorium door has the treasurer marked in from five past eight to nine for the act run.","category":"record","source":"Loading bay book","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Three things left the building inside the window, and the book cannot say with whom. The board has the treasurer in the stalls throughout, if the board is right.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Director on the talkback feed","description":"Statement of Hollis Grey, director: he had the office monitor fed from the booth talkback last season so he could hear the desk during notes, and nobody switches it off.","category":"testimony","source":"Director''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"A voice from that speaker is a voice from the booth.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Cue computer log","description":"Cue computer log: eighteen GO presses from the booth desk between 20:02 and 20:40; cues nine to eleven at 20:08, 20:11 and 20:13; a red pen found under the equipment rack beside the desk, its cap still on the desk.","category":"record","source":"Cue computer log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Someone was pressing cues at the desk through the window, including the three ticked in black, and the red pen was on the floor where she says it went.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Loading bay camera still","description":"Loading bay camera, 20:26: the treasurer wheels the costume hamper out, a white envelope tucked under the lid strap; the stalls board by the auditorium door still shows the treasurer marked in.","category":"record","source":"Loading bay camera","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The envelope left in the hamper, in the treasurer''s hands, while the board said stalls.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Recovered file from the booth computer","description":"Booth computer recycle bin: \"act2_lead_take3\", nine minutes of Tavi singing the lead''s second act number over the talkback, saved 20:22.","category":"record","source":"Booth computer","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The deleted file is a singing take, timed inside the window, from the booth.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Director on the deleted file","description":"Statement of Hollis Grey, director: as soon as rehearsal ended, Tavi deleted a file from the booth computer and went red when he asked about it.","category":"red_herring","source":"Director''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The file was her singing the lead''s part through the talkback. It is in the recycle bin.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"How was your voice in the office at ten past eight when you were upstairs?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Walk me through the second act run from eight o''clock.","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"Who counts the takings, and who''s got a key to that office?","category":"access","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Why''d you delete a file the second rehearsal ended?","category":"evidence","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Why do cues nine to eleven switch to black ink?","category":"timeline","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"How would you get from the booth to the office?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"What were you saying into the talkback while the speaker was live?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-2","why":"Her voice was in the office, and the talkback record shows where it came from."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The voice in the office came from the booth, which is where Tavi says she was."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"Cue presses from the desk every couple of minutes keep her in the booth, black-pen cues included."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The camera has the envelope leaving in the hamper with the treasurer."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"The deleted file was her singing, recovered intact; it has nothing to do with the cash box."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Why could people hear your voice in the office?","Is there a speaker in the office?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The office monitor carries the booth talkback."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Where were you at twenty past eight?","Can anything prove you were in the booth?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She was pressing cues at the desk through the window."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Is there another way down from the booth?","Who would have seen you leave the booth?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The booth stairs come down past the stage manager''s desk, so a trip to the office would have been seen."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["Who has an office key?","Who sealed the envelope?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-5"],"why":"Two people hold office keys, and the camera shows which of them carried the envelope out."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='2e17a8ab633949a5a5a324ad7434cd55a773eae4f331d52f00b577dd6ce9b923');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"59c85fe8-fae9-4a38-af5a-f60da7fdbce1","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Salted Dye Vat","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Northgate Mills textile cooperative","crime":"The first full batch of the cooperative''s new salt-free indigo came out of vat three with a faded stripe round it and tested high for salt.","difficulty":"medium","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"8a81728c2836595f18e405d80c7a488cefca48253d4af4aa7ff8dc00a3db379a","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Vat three at Northgate Mills carried the first full run of the new salt-free indigo on Tuesday night. The cloth came out at eight with a faded stripe running right round it, and the lab says the bath held salt where the recipe allows none. Salt in an indigo bath bleaches the cloth where the surface of the dye touches it, so a stripe at one height means the salt went in part way through the run. The lid seal was intact. Low on the vat''s side is the sampling port, a screw-capped spout for drawing a cupful of the bath to test without lifting the lid. Orin Vale did the scheduled sample check at half past six and wrote up the gauge reading, leaving empty the box where the technician records whether the port was opened. A junior weaver carried two scoops of bulk salt past vat three for the fixing bath. The technical lead wants to know whether Orin salted that vat.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Orin Vale","ageRange":"fifties","role":"dye technician","avatarDescription":"Indigo stains to the elbows, cropped grey hair, a dye-spotted notebook in his shirt pocket.","speechStyle":"Trade jargon and long pauses. Ends a lot of sentences with \"see\".","attitude":"Anxious and sorry for it, wipes his hands on his apron between answers and apologises for the pauses.","relationship":"Twenty-two years at the vats; the salt-free process replaced the recipe he wrote, and the technical lead who signed it off trained under him."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"During the half past six sample check Orin unscrewed the sampling port cap on vat three and tipped one measured salt bag from his calibration kit into the bath, then logged the gauge and left the port column blank.","motive":"He believes the salt-free process makes his twenty-two years of craft worthless, and a failed first run would put his own recipe back on the vats before his contract review.","opportunity":"The scheduled sample check gave him two minutes alone at the open port.","means":"The sampling port is a screw cap below the lid seal, and a five hundred gram measured bag in the two hundred litre bath matches the salt concentration the lab found.","realAlibi":"I was at vat three around half past six taking the scheduled sample, then back at the bench writing it up.","explanation":"The sampling port sits outside the lid seal. The vat data logger shows a forty-second pressure dip at twenty-seven minutes to seven, the signature of the port cap off, against Orin''s claim that he only read the gauge. His calibration kit is one measured bag short, and one bag in the two hundred litre bath gives exactly the one part in four hundred on the lab sheet. The weaver''s two scoops went into the fixing bath and are on the finishing team''s sheet, weighed in whole, and she was on the hoist when the port opened; she had applied for his post, and her cup held rinse water. The canteen remark is opinion, which half the floor shares.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-01-27T17:30:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T18:20:00Z","location":"dye floor bench","description":"Orin writes up the afternoon samples at the bench by vat three.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-01-27T18:28:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T18:40:00Z","location":"vat three","description":"Orin takes the scheduled sample, unscrews the port cap and tips a measured salt bag through.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-01-27T18:40:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T19:30:00Z","location":"dye floor bench","description":"Orin logs the gauge reading, leaves the port column blank and carries on with paperwork.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-01-27T21:30:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T21:31:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Orin sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"vat three","from":"2026-01-27T08:00:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T22:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The sampling port is a screw cap below the lid seal, so it opens without breaking the seal.","disclosurePhrases":["The sample port sits under the seal line.","You can open the port and the seal stays whole.","The port cap comes off and the seal stays whole.","The seal only covers the lid, see."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I had the sampling port cap off during the half past six check.","disclosurePhrases":["I had the port cap off at half six.","I did open the port during the check.","The port was open while I took the sample.","I unscrewed the cap for the sample."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"One of the twelve measured salt bags is gone from my calibration kit.","disclosurePhrases":["There''s a salt bag gone from my kit.","My kit''s down to eleven bags.","One of my measured bags is missing.","The kit should have twelve bags and it''s got eleven."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I warned the technical lead in December that the new process would make my job a training exercise.","disclosurePhrases":["I warned them the new process makes me a trainee again.","The new process makes twenty years of mine worthless.","I''d argued against the new process back in December.","I put it to the lead that the process would finish me."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The old indigo recipe the new process replaced is mine, and a failed run would put it back on the vats.","disclosurePhrases":["The old recipe is mine, see.","A failed run puts the old recipe back on.","They''d go back to my recipe.","The old process is the one I wrote."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-6","statement":"I was back at the bench by twenty to seven writing up, where the weaver saw me, and stayed there to the end of the shift.","disclosurePhrases":["I was back at the bench by twenty to seven.","The weaver saw me at the bench.","I sat at the bench writing up till the shift end.","From twenty to seven I was at the bench."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"doesNotKnow":["What the weaver saw or said.","Whether the lab kept a second sample.","What the finishing team logged."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I had the sampling port cap off during the half past six check.","permittedDeception":"I may say I only read the gauge at half past six and that the port cap stayed on, until the data logger is put to me."},{"factId":"fact-4","realFact":"I warned the technical lead in December that the new process would make my job a training exercise.","permittedDeception":"I may say I welcomed the new process until the detective presses on the canteen remark or my December meeting with the technical lead."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Faded stripe and the lid seal","description":"Scene notes: a faded stripe runs round the cloth a hand''s width below where it was clipped to the frame, at the height where the bath surface sat once the cloth had taken up dye for an hour; the lid seal strip on vat three is unbroken.","category":"physical","source":"Scene notes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The stripe is where the surface of the bath met the cloth in the second hour, so whatever went in went in during the run, and the lid was never lifted.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Vat three log, late shift","description":"Vat log, late shift: run started at half past five, cloth out at eight; sample check at half past six, entered by Orin; gauge reading normal; the \"port opened\" box left empty.","category":"record","source":"Vat log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Puts Orin at the vat in the window. An empty box is an empty box.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Lab sheet on the retained sample","description":"Lab sheet: the retained sample from vat three, drawn by the night lab at 20:00 from the bath as the cloth came out, carries salt at one part in four hundred; Monday''s sample from the same vat carried none.","category":"record","source":"Lab sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"A measurable quantity, not a trace. Someone added a known amount, or a known amount got in.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Junior weaver on the salt and the sink","description":"Junior weaver''s statement: at about a quarter to seven she carried two scoops of bulk salt past vat three to the fixing bath, rinsed the cup at the sink beside the vat, and saw Orin writing at the bench.","category":"testimony","source":"Junior weaver''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Loose salt went past that vat in the window in someone else''s hands, and Orin was at the bench when it did.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Vat three drawings","description":"Vat drawings: the sampling port on vat three is a screw cap set below the lid line, outside the seal strip, wide enough to pour through.","category":"record","source":"Vat drawings","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The seal proves nothing about the port, and the port takes more than a sample cup.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Data logger trace for vat three","description":"Vat data logger: a 40-second pressure dip at 18:33, the trace the port gives when its cap is off; a sample draw through the port shows as a dip of under ten seconds, as on Monday''s trace at 18:31; flat through the rest of the shift.","category":"record","source":"Vat data logger","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The port was open during his check, four times longer than a sample takes, and at no other time, against his account of reading the gauge only.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Calibration kit count","description":"Kit inventory, counted at nine: eleven measured salt bags of twelve in Orin''s calibration kit, each bag five hundred grams; vat three holds two hundred litres at the run mark.","category":"physical","source":"Kit inventory","kind":"observation","interpretation":"One five hundred gram bag in two hundred litres is one part in four hundred, which is the lab figure.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Process file for the old recipe","description":"Process file: the superseded indigo recipe, signed O. Vale, marked \"withdrawn, replaced by salt-free process\" in the technical lead''s hand, dated October.","category":"record","source":"Process file","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The recipe that comes back if the new one fails is his.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}},{"id":"record-5","title":"Finishing team sheet and the weaver''s file","description":"Finishing team sheet: two scoops of bulk salt, four hundred grams, weighed into the fixing bath at 18:47 by the junior weaver, matching the four hundred grams drawn from the salt bin at 18:42; the hoist log has her on the hoist from 18:25 to 18:41. Her staff file holds an application, lodged in December, for the dye technician''s post should Orin''s contract lapse in March.","category":"record","source":"Finishing team sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"She had a reason to want his run to fail and salt in her hands, and every gram of it went into the fixing bath while she was on the hoist and the port was open.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-6"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Junior weaver on the canteen","description":"Junior weaver''s statement: on Monday in the canteen Orin called the new process a waste of good indigo, loud enough for the next table.","category":"red_herring","source":"Junior weaver''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Half the floor has said the same. Opinion is a reason to ask, and nothing more.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Could anything have gone into that bath without lifting the lid?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"What did the half past six sample check involve?","category":"timeline","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What happens to the old recipe if the new one fails?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"What do you make of the new process?","category":"motive","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Does one part in four hundred mean anything to you?","category":"evidence","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"What was in your hands while the cap was off?","category":"access","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Who opened the port tonight?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"Where were you between the check and the end of the shift?","category":"alibi","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-6"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"A known quantity of salt went in, which rules out residue and points at a measured amount."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The logger has the port open during his check and at no other time, against his claim that only the gauge was read."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"His kit is one bag short, and that bag is exactly the lab figure."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-5","why":"A grumble in the canteen is shared by half the floor and moves no salt."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Is there any way into the vat with the lid sealed?","Could anything get past the seal?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The port sits outside the seal."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Was the port cap off during your check?","What did you do at the vat at half six?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He claims gauge only, then concedes the cap was off when the logger is put to him."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Is your calibration kit complete?","Where is the twelfth salt bag?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"His kit is a measured bag short, which matches the lab figure."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='8a81728c2836595f18e405d80c7a488cefca48253d4af4aa7ff8dc00a3db379a');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"35e4837c-46c1-44ef-a4ef-86a50d3f2476","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Twenty Past Ten at the Dome","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Dunmere public observatory","crime":"Someone switched off the main telescope''s tracking drive at the breaker during the Thursday public session, wrecking a visiting astronomer''s two-hour photographic exposure.","difficulty":"hard","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"19f2e47498bcacdb52a4bb41c18f0b8ff85f8c4c7a5cc1792129ee5ce0c64445","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Thursday was the last clear night of the season at Dunmere, and the main telescope was on a two-hour exposure for a visiting astronomer whose university is deciding whether to fund the observatory''s public programme. At ten past ten the tracking drive stopped. The controller logged no fault; the breaker in the drive cabinet had been thrown by hand. Sela Moss had the last dome tour, eleven visitors, and signed the dome sheet to say they were off the floor at ten. They were still on the floor at twenty past, a few feet from the cabinet, and the astronomer heard a woman explaining the mount beside it when the hum died. Sela has argued for a year that the late research bookings ruin her tours, and the board has heard her say so in writing. Eleven people stood round her when the drive stopped, and the question is whether her hand was on that breaker.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Sela Moss","ageRange":"sixties","role":"visitor guide","avatarDescription":"Quilted coat buttoned to the chin and a small torch on a lanyard that she clicks on and off while she listens.","speechStyle":"Full, old-fashioned sentences, no slang, pauses to choose a word.","attitude":"Formal and faintly superior, corrects small errors in the question before answering, and finds being kept more funny than annoying.","relationship":"Volunteer guide for nine years and the loudest voice against the late research bookings that have been cutting her tours short."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"caretaker","actualEvent":"The caretaker came through the service door onto the landing of the service stair at eight minutes past ten, opened the drive cabinet''s rear access panel from the stair side with the key on the caretaker''s ring, threw the tracking breaker at ten past, and left the way they had come at twelve past.","motive":"The caretaker is on a fixed stipend that stops at ten, and the late research bookings keep the building open past midnight unpaid; the board refused the caretaker''s request in May to end them, and a ruined exposure looked like the way to end them.","opportunity":"The caretaker holds the only cabinet key and uses the service door all night without anyone noticing.","means":"The cabinet is let into the wall between the dome floor and the service stair and opens from both sides; the stair-side panel takes the key on the caretaker''s ring, and the breaker leaves no fault code.","realAlibi":"I was on the dome floor with eleven visitors, beside the mount, explaining the gearing when the drive stopped at about ten past ten.","explanation":"The breaker was thrown by hand, and the only cabinet key is on the caretaker''s ring. The cabinet opens from the service stair as well as the dome floor, and the stair-side panel was the one found unlatched. Guides sign the dome sheet at the planned finish so the till can close, and the desk log has the real time. A visitor''s phone video has Sela at the mount with the handout in one hand and her torch on the gearing in the other, the dome-side cabinet door shut and latched behind her, when the hum stops. The door log shows the caretaker''s fob opening the service door at eight minutes past and again at twelve past, and the caretaker''s refused letter to the board is in the same folder. The torch beam the astronomer saw was on the mount gearing, which stands in front of the cabinet, and the cold visitor was in the car park in sight of the desk.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-18T21:30:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T22:00:00Z","location":"dome floor","description":"Sela leads eleven visitors round the main telescope.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-18T22:00:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T22:20:00Z","location":"dome floor","description":"Sela runs over time, stalls on a constellation name, and is explaining the mount gearing when the drive stops.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"caretaker","at":"2026-06-18T22:08:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T22:12:00Z","location":"service stair landing","description":"The caretaker comes through the service door, opens the cabinet''s rear panel from the stair and throws the tracking breaker.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"caretaker","at":"2026-06-18T22:12:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T22:20:00Z","location":"boiler room","description":"The caretaker goes back down to the boiler room.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-18T22:45:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T22:46:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Sela sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"dome floor","from":"2026-06-18T21:00:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T23:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"caretaker","location":"service stair landing","from":"2026-06-18T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-06-18T23:59:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The drive cabinet key is on the caretaker''s ring, and guides only carry the dome door key.","disclosurePhrases":["I''ve only ever had the dome door key.","The cabinet key is on the caretaker''s ring.","Guides are given the door key and that''s all.","The cabinet has always been the caretaker''s to open."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I was beside the mount explaining the gearing to the group when the hum stopped, and one visitor was filming.","disclosurePhrases":["I was mid-sentence at the mount when it went quiet.","One of the visitors was filming me when the hum stopped.","I had the whole group round the mount at the time.","The drive went quiet while I was talking."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"I heard the service door and the caretaker''s keys on the stair behind the cabinet a minute or two before the drive stopped.","disclosurePhrases":["I heard the service door go behind the wall.","I heard the keys before the drive stopped.","The caretaker was on the stair just before it went quiet.","The service door opened a minute before the hum stopped."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"Guides sign the dome sheet at the planned finish, before the tour, so the desk can close the till.","disclosurePhrases":["I sign the sheet at the planned finish.","The dome sheet gets signed before the tour, for the desk.","I always sign off at ten so the desk can close.","The ten o''clock signature is just for the till."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The tour ran late because I forgot the name of a constellation and had to find it on my handout.","disclosurePhrases":["I forgot the name of a constellation.","I went blank on a constellation name.","I had to check my handout for a name.","I''m afraid I lost a name and stalled."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-6","statement":"The university partnership would pay for the public programme, and my post goes with the programme.","disclosurePhrases":["The partnership money pays for the public nights.","My post goes with the public programme.","The board would close the public nights over this.","The public programme lives on that partnership money."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"doesNotKnow":["Why the caretaker came up the stair.","Whether the door log records fobs.","What the astronomer has said."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1","fact-5"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-6"],"misleading":[]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Drive controller log","description":"Drive controller: tracking lost 22:10; breaker found in the off position when restored at 22:20; no fault code recorded. Boiler room log, in the caretaker''s hand: boiler checked 22:00 and 22:30, \"in boiler room between\".","category":"record","source":"Drive controller","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Nothing failed. A person moved the breaker, which is behind a cabinet door, and the man who holds its key has written himself two floors down.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Dome sheet signed by Sela","description":"Visitor desk: the dome sheet, signed S. Moss, gives her tour as off the floor at ten; the desk logged the group out at twenty past.","category":"record","source":"Visitor desk","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Her own signature says the group left before the drive stopped. It did not.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Astronomer on the voice by the cabinet","description":"Astronomer''s statement: a woman''s voice was explaining the mount a few feet from the cabinet when the drive hum stopped.","category":"testimony","source":"Astronomer''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Puts Sela near the cabinet and talking. Talking is not opening a cabinet.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Drive cabinet as found","description":"Cabinet notes: the drive cabinet is let into the wall between the dome floor and the service stair and opens from either side with the same key; the dome-side door is an arm''s length from where the group stood round the mount; at twenty past ten the breaker inside was off and the key was in neither lock. Guides practise the breaker reset in their spring training, Sela among them.","category":"physical","source":"Cabinet notes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Whoever threw it had a key and reached the breaker from one side of the wall or the other. Sela knew where it was and stood within reach of one door.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Key register for the dome","description":"Key register: drive cabinet key, one copy, held on the caretaker''s ring; guides are issued the dome door key only; the service stair is reached by the fobbed service door from the boiler room side, or past the visitor desk.","category":"record","source":"Key register","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Sela could stand beside the cabinet all night and never open it, from either side.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Visitor''s phone video","description":"Visitor''s phone video, nine minutes: Sela beside the mount, handout in one hand and her torch on the gearing in the other, the dome-side cabinet door shut and latched behind her; the drive hum stops at the seven minute mark and she looks up at the telescope.","category":"record","source":"Visitor''s phone","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Her hands are full and the dome-side door is latched when the hum stops. The breaker was reached from the other side of the wall.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Service door fob log and the caretaker''s letter","description":"Door log: the service door onto the stair landing opened on the caretaker''s fob at 22:08 and again at 22:12. Filed with the printout, the caretaker''s letter to the board from May: the stipend stops at ten, the research bookings run past midnight unpaid, and the caretaker asks that they end; the chair''s reply across the foot of it reads \"refused\".","category":"record","source":"Door log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The person with the cabinet key was on the stair behind it for the four minutes that matter, and had asked the board to end the bookings the exposure was for.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Desk note on signing the dome sheet","description":"Visitor desk note, pinned above the till: \"Guides sign the dome sheet at the planned finish so the till can close. Real end time goes in the desk log.\" Desk log: last tour out at twenty past.","category":"record","source":"Visitor desk","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The signed ten o''clock is a till convention. The desk log has her still on the floor.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-5","title":"Cabinet latches as found","description":"Cabinet notes, second page: at twenty past ten the dome-side door was shut and latched; the stair-side panel was pushed to but not latched; the fobbed service door is the only way onto that stair from the boiler room side without passing the desk.","category":"physical","source":"Cabinet notes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The side left open is the stair side, which the group could not reach and the caretaker could, and his own log has him below it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Astronomer on the torch beam","description":"Astronomer''s statement: in the minute before the hum stopped, a torch beam from where the woman stood swept across the drive cabinet door and back.","category":"red_herring","source":"Astronomer''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Guides light the mount gearing with the torch during the talk, and the cabinet stands behind the mount; the video has the beam on the gearing and the cabinet door shut behind it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-6","title":"Desk volunteer on the visitor who left early","description":"Visitor desk: one visitor complained of the cold, signed out of the dome at ten and went off alone before the rest of the group.","category":"red_herring","source":"Visitor desk","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The visitor went to the car park and was in sight of the desk until half past.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Could you have opened the drive cabinet?","category":"access","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Where were you standing when the drive stopped?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What do the late bookings bring in?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-6"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Your tour ran twenty minutes over. Why?","category":"timeline","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Why sign the sheet to say the tour ended at ten?","category":"evidence","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Did anyone else come near the dome floor while you had the group round the mount?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Where was the caretaker at ten past?","category":"access","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"No fault means a hand on the breaker, behind a locked cabinet door."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The only cabinet key is on the caretaker''s ring, which Sela has never held."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The video has her hands on the handout and the torch, and the dome-side door latched, when the hum stops."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The caretaker''s fob was on the stair behind the cabinet for exactly the minutes that matter, against his own boiler log, with a refused request to end the bookings on file."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"The torch beam was on the mount gearing, which stands in front of the cabinet; the video has the door shut behind her."},{"evidenceId":"exhibit-6","why":"The cold visitor was at the car park, in sight of the desk, when the drive stopped."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Who has the key to the drive cabinet?","What keys do guides carry?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"Guides carry the door key only; the cabinet key is the caretaker''s."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["What were you doing at ten past ten?","Was anyone watching you when the hum stopped?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She was mid-explanation at the mount and a visitor was filming."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Did the service door open during your tour?","Who else was near the dome?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The caretaker came onto the stair behind the cabinet a minute before the drive stopped."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='19f2e47498bcacdb52a4bb41c18f0b8ff85f8c4c7a5cc1792129ee5ce0c64445');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"2cbaa411-6440-46e6-a119-9857998668d0","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Silent Ferry Bell","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Mallowreach canal ferry depot","crime":"The Kestrel''s departure bell was wedged silent before its half past seven test, and the crew forfeited the first slot of the season to the Heron.","difficulty":"easy","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"0f2dbbf6961dacd2968771d6a9fbf21dc440b190e5db89208c6f339d9fc4c40c","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Two crews share the Mallowreach depot and the Kestrel had Monday''s first slot, the first of the season. The departure bell works like a doorbell: the deckhand presses the test button, a coil in the cabinet throws a steel striker at the bell, and a green lamp on the quay lights whenever the coil gets power. At the half past seven test the lamp lit and nobody on the quay heard the bell, so under depot rules the slot went to the Heron and the Kestrel sat tied up for an hour. When the cabinet was opened afterwards the striker travelled a finger''s width and met something soft. Bren Ash writes the maintenance rota for both boats, signed the Kestrel''s bell cabinet off at five past seven, and crews on the Heron, where his kit bag was handed up ten minutes later. The Kestrel''s deckhand heard the cabinet door bang at twenty past seven, with Bren already across the water, and turned round to an empty quay. Bren''s signature calls the striker sound at five past seven. Was it?","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Bren Ash","ageRange":"early forties","role":"maintenance scheduler","avatarDescription":"An oilskin jacket and a depot beanie pulled low over a face that has spent twenty winters on the canal.","speechStyle":"Boat words and long answers that set off somewhere and come back by a different route.","attitude":"Hearty and expansive, laughs before the question is finished and calls the detective \"skipper\".","relationship":"Writes the rota for both crews and crews on the Heron himself; the Heron''s skipper carried him through the winter with a loan, and the whole depot knows it."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"During the five past seven cabinet check Bren lifted the Kestrel''s striker arm out of its cradle, fitted a grey felt pad from his maintenance kit beneath it, signed the sheet \"all sound\", left the latch on its first notch with the tag wire unthreaded, and carried his kit bag over to the Heron.","motive":"He had promised the Heron crew the first slot of the season on Sunday night, with its bonus, and the only way to deliver it was for the Kestrel to fail its test.","opportunity":"The scheduled cabinet sign-off put him alone at the open cabinet twenty-five minutes before the test.","means":"A felt pad under the striker lets the coil fire and the lamp light while the bell stays silent.","realAlibi":"I was at the Kestrel''s bell cabinet about five past seven doing the sign-off, then over to the Heron with my kit bag.","explanation":"The lamp sits on the coil circuit, so a padded striker passes the electrical test and fails the bell. The dock camera has Bren at the open cabinet at four minutes past seven with the striker arm in his hand, against his claim that he only looked. The felt pad carries the batch stamp of the kit issued to the scheduler in January, and the Heron crew''s Sunday message has him promising them the slot. The tag wire was never threaded through the latch, as the camera shows at nine minutes past, so it is unbroken and the door could blow open; the bang at twenty past was the wind, and the camera shows the door swinging with nobody near it. The Heron''s skipper had asked for the slot in writing and was aboard with the gangway up from ten to seven.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-30T06:45:00Z","until":"2026-03-30T07:00:00Z","location":"dock office","description":"Bren collects the inspection sheets and his kit bag.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-30T07:05:00Z","until":"2026-03-30T07:10:00Z","location":"Kestrel bell cabinet","description":"Bren opens the cabinet, lifts the striker arm, fits a grey felt pad beneath it, signs the sheet and leaves the latch on its first notch with the tag wire hanging.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-30T07:12:00Z","until":"2026-03-30T07:20:00Z","location":"Heron berth","description":"Bren hands his kit bag up to the Heron''s deckhand.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-30T07:30:00Z","until":"2026-03-30T07:35:00Z","location":"dock office","description":"Bren watches the Kestrel''s silent test from the office window.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-30T08:15:00Z","until":"2026-03-30T08:16:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Bren sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"Kestrel bell cabinet","from":"2026-03-30T06:00:00Z","until":"2026-03-30T09:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The indicator lamp lights off the coil, so a blocked striker still leaves the lamp on.","disclosurePhrases":["The lamp will light with the striker blocked.","That lamp only tells you the coil fired.","A jammed striker still lights the lamp.","The lamp''s on the coil, the bell''s on the arm."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I lifted the striker arm out of its cradle during the five past seven check.","disclosurePhrases":["I did have the striker arm out.","I lifted the striker during the check.","The arm was out of the cradle while I looked.","I had the striker in my hand at five past."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"My maintenance kit carries grey felt pads from the January issue.","disclosurePhrases":["My kit carries grey felt pads.","Those grey pads came with my kit in January.","There is grey felt in every scheduler kit.","I keep felt pads in the kit for the latches."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I promised the Heron crew the first slot of the season on Sunday night.","disclosurePhrases":["I promised the Heron lads the slot.","I promised my crew the first slot.","The Heron crew had my word on the slot.","I''d given the Heron the first slot in my head."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The first slot of the season carries the crew bonus, about a week''s wages a head.","disclosurePhrases":["First slot comes with the season bonus.","The first boat out gets the bonus money.","There''s a week''s wages riding on that slot.","The slot''s worth a bonus to the crew."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["Whether the dock camera was recording.","What either deckhand has said."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I lifted the striker arm out of its cradle during the five past seven check.","permittedDeception":"I may say I only opened the cabinet door for a look and never laid a hand on the striker, until the dock camera is put to me."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Departure test result","description":"Dock test log: 07:30 test on the Kestrel, indicator lamp lit, bell silent, slot forfeited to the Heron under depot rules; the scheduler''s sign-off on a cabinet that failed inside the hour referred to the depot manager for review, as the rota rules require.","category":"record","source":"Dock test log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The circuit worked and the bell did not. Who gained is on the same line, and the man who signed the cabinet off is on the line below, with his rota job in question.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Bell cabinet inspection sheet","description":"Dock office: the Kestrel bell cabinet inspection sheet is signed B. Ash, five past seven, \"all sound, latched and tagged\".","category":"record","source":"Dock office","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Bren was in that cabinet twenty-five minutes before the test and signed it good, tag and all.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Cabinet latch and striker travel","description":"Cabinet notes: the latch is a two-notch catch with a tamper tag wire that threads through it once it is home on the second notch; the latch was found resting on its first notch with the tag wire hanging loose and unbroken; a wire draws back out of a first-notch latch without breaking; the striker travels a finger''s width and meets something soft.","category":"physical","source":"Cabinet notes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Nobody forced the cabinet. Either it was never tagged, or someone after him eased the latch back a notch and slid the wire out.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Heron deckhand on the kit bag","description":"Deckhand''s statement: the Heron''s deckhand took Bren''s kit bag over the rail at about a quarter past seven and stowed it in the wheelhouse locker.","category":"testimony","source":"Deckhand''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"His kit went to the Heron straight after the cabinet. What was in it is the question.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Bell circuit drawing","description":"Circuit drawing: the indicator lamp sits on the coil; the striker arm is mechanical and has no sensor of its own.","category":"record","source":"Circuit drawing","kind":"observation","interpretation":"A lit lamp proves the coil fired and nothing about the arm.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Dock camera stills, seven to half past","description":"Dock camera: 07:06, Bren at the open Kestrel cabinet with the striker arm lifted in his left hand; 07:09, Bren pushing the door to with the tag wire hanging outside the latch; 07:20, the cabinet door swinging open in the wind with nobody on the quay, and nobody at it between.","category":"record","source":"Dock camera","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Against his claim of looking only. The arm was out, in his hand, the tag never went through, and the later bang was the wind on an untagged door.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Felt pad from the striker","description":"Cabinet notes: the pad under the striker is grey felt stamped with kit batch MK-7, the batch issued to the scheduler''s maintenance kit in January; the Kestrel''s own kit carries batch MK-4.","category":"physical","source":"Cabinet notes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The thing that silenced the bell came from his kit, and from no other kit on the quay.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Heron crew message from Sunday","description":"Crew group message, Sunday 21:48, from Bren: \"first slot''s ours tomorrow, trust me\".","category":"testimony","source":"Crew group message","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He promised a result he could only deliver by stopping the Kestrel.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-5","title":"Heron skipper''s slot request","description":"Dock office: a note from the Heron''s skipper on Friday asking the harbourmaster to swap the first slot to the Heron, marked refused and initialled; the dock camera has the skipper aboard the Heron from 06:50 to the test with the gangway up, and nobody but Bren at the Kestrel cabinet between seven and half past.","category":"record","source":"Dock office","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The skipper wanted the slot badly enough to ask in writing, and could not have reached the cabinet. One pair of hands was in it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Kestrel deckhand on the door","description":"Deckhand''s statement: the Kestrel''s deckhand was coiling lines on the foredeck at about twenty past seven when the bell cabinet door banged on the quay behind him; he turned round to an empty quay, with Bren already over at the Heron''s berth.","category":"red_herring","source":"Deckhand''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The dock camera has the door swinging in the wind on the first notch of the latch, with the quay empty.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"How can the lamp light while the bell stays silent?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Walk me through the cabinet check at five past seven.","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What''s riding on the first slot?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"What did you promise the Heron lads for this season?","category":"motive","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Why did your kit bag go onto the Heron straight after the check?","category":"timeline","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"What went back into the cradle with the striker?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"He was in the cabinet, alone and by right, twenty-five minutes before the silent test, and signed it tagged when it was not."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The camera has the striker in his hand, against his claim that he only looked, and shows the later bang was the wind."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The pad that silenced the bell carries his kit''s batch stamp and no other kit''s."},{"evidenceId":"record-4","why":"He promised his crew the slot the night before, a result only a failed test could give."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The door bang was the wind on an unlatched, untagged door; the camera shows the quay empty."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Why would the light come on and the bell stay quiet?","Was it a power fault?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The lamp is on the coil; the fault is mechanical, in the striker."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Did you touch the striker during the check?","What did the inspection involve?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He claims a look only, then concedes the striker was out when the camera is put to him."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Where did that felt come from?","Is that pad from your kit?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"His kit carries the grey felt, and the pad''s batch stamp is his kit''s."},{"questionId":"question-4","alternativePhrasings":["Did you promise the Heron crew anything?","Who wanted the first slot?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"He had promised his crew the slot the night before."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='0f2dbbf6961dacd2968771d6a9fbf21dc440b190e5db89208c6f339d9fc4c40c');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"8ed84e64-fa1f-45e8-a93f-a7388e443169","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Cold Pantry Ledger","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Elmbrook communal bakery","crime":"The competition recipe ledger went from its shelf in the cold pantry between half past nine and ten on the morning of the judging walk-round.","difficulty":"medium","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"2f6df772ae98799182f5704373d740d6bbd8422615610146edccbe9643231975","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"The competition ledger at Elmbrook, a red-spined book holding every entrant''s recipe, was on its shelf in the cold pantry at half past nine on Saturday when the head baker fetched butter. By ten the shelf was bare. In between, the judge did the walk-round, the pantry delivery door opened four times, and two collections went out with folders. Josh Wren, second-year apprentice and an entrant himself, signed into the cooling room at twenty past nine and out again at ten past ten, and between those two entries is a pantry line he has scratched out. There is a torn corner of lined paper in his apron pocket, and he kept the head baker away from a covered box in the cooling room. The final bake is tomorrow and the entrants want their recipes back. Josh has already asked twice whether this will take long.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Josh Wren","ageRange":"twenty-four","role":"apprentice baker","avatarDescription":"Flour-dusted apron and a bandana, with a smear of icing sugar he has not noticed on one cheek.","speechStyle":"Jokes first, answers second, and says sorry when the joke lands wrong.","attitude":"Cheerful and jokey, slow to take anything seriously, visibly short of sleep.","relationship":"Second-year apprentice to the head baker and an entrant in the competition himself; his parents are driving over for the final and have told half the village."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"judge","actualEvent":"During the stock check at twenty to ten the visiting judge, Mr Dray, slid the red-spined ledger into one of two delivery folders waiting on the pantry crate, and carried that folder out through the delivery door at five to ten.","motive":"The judge''s nephew is an entrant, and the judge wanted him to see the leading recipe before the final bake.","opportunity":"The stock check put the judge alone in the cold pantry with the shelf and the waiting folders.","means":"An oversized delivery folder, one of two leaving at the same time, hid the narrow ledger.","realAlibi":"I was in the cooling room from about twenty past nine until after ten, icing a practice cake for my parents'' anniversary.","explanation":"The connecting door between the cooling room and the pantry has been bolted on the pantry side since the inspection, so the only way from the icing table to the ledger shelf is round through the bakehouse past the head baker at the ovens. The flour rep waited at the cooling room window from twenty-five past nine until ten for his folder and watched Josh pipe the whole border. The scratched pantry line was a reminder to fetch butter, which the head baker passed through the door at half past before he went for it. The scrap in his apron is the torn corner of his own practice recipe card, which is in the cake box with the anniversary cake. The stock camera has the judge lifting the ledger from the shelf into a folder at twenty to ten, and the entry list has the judge''s surname against entrant seven.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-14T08:30:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T09:15:00Z","location":"bakehouse","description":"Josh finishes the morning batch at the ovens.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-14T09:20:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T10:10:00Z","location":"cooling room","description":"Josh ices a practice anniversary cake, tearing a corner off his recipe card to wipe the nozzle, and scratches out his butter reminder when the head baker passes a block through the door.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"judge","at":"2026-02-14T09:38:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T09:44:00Z","location":"cold pantry","description":"The visiting judge slides the ledger into a delivery folder during the stock check.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"judge","at":"2026-02-14T09:52:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T09:56:00Z","location":"delivery door","description":"The judge leaves through the delivery door with the folder.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-14T10:40:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T10:41:00Z","location":"interview room","description":"Josh sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"cooling room","from":"2026-02-14T08:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T12:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"judge","location":"cold pantry","from":"2026-02-14T09:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T11:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"judge","location":"delivery door","from":"2026-02-14T09:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-14T11:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The ledger shelf is just inside the delivery door, and the door between the cooling room and the pantry has been bolted on the pantry side since the inspection.","disclosurePhrases":["That shelf is right by the delivery door.","The ledger lives by the back door, by the deliveries.","The pantry door from the cooling room is bolted their side.","You''d have to go round through the bakehouse to reach it."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I was in the cooling room from about twenty past nine until after ten, and the scratched pantry line was a butter reminder I did not need.","disclosurePhrases":["I was in the cooling room till after ten.","I went into the cooling room around twenty past nine.","The pantry line was a note to myself for butter.","The cooling room is where I was."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The torn corner in my apron came off my practice recipe card.","disclosurePhrases":["That corner''s off my recipe card.","The paper in my pocket is from my own recipe.","I tore the corner off my practice card.","It''s a bit of my recipe card, to be fair."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-5"]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"The judge did the stock check in the pantry at about twenty to ten, and I heard the door go through the wall.","disclosurePhrases":["The judge was in the pantry around twenty to ten.","I heard the pantry door go while the judge was checking stock.","The stock check was happening next door to me.","The judge''s stock check was at twenty to ten."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"I was icing a surprise anniversary cake for my parents in the cooling room, and the flour rep stood at the window watching me do it.","disclosurePhrases":["I was icing a cake for my parents'' anniversary.","The box has got my parents'' anniversary cake in it.","It''s a surprise cake for my mum and dad.","The flour rep watched me pipe the whole border."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-6","statement":"Every entrant''s recipe is in the ledger, and the winner gets a year''s flour contract with the mill.","disclosurePhrases":["The winner''s recipe gets the mill contract.","There''s a year''s contract riding on that book.","Every entrant''s recipe is in that ledger.","Whoever wins gets the mill''s flour contract."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"doesNotKnow":["What the judge did in the pantry.","Which folder went where.","Whether the pantry has a camera.","Who the judge is related to."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-5","realFact":"I was icing a surprise anniversary cake for my parents in the cooling room, and the flour rep stood at the window watching me do it.","permittedDeception":"I may say I was cleaning the cooling room and that the covered box holds leftovers, until the detective asks straight what is in the box or presses twice."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Bare ledger shelf","description":"Pantry walk: the labelled ledger shelf is bare; the dust line shows the book was drawn straight out, with nothing else on the shelf moved.","category":"physical","source":"Pantry walk","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Someone who knew the shelf took the book and nothing else.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Cooling room sheet","description":"Cooling room sheet, signed J. Wren: in at twenty past nine, out at ten past ten; between the two a line reading \"pantry, butter\" with the time scratched out.","category":"record","source":"Cooling room sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"His own sheet has a pantry entry in the window, crossed out. Either he went and thought better of recording it, or he wrote a note he did not act on.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Torn paper in the apron","description":"Apron find: a torn corner of lined paper, floury, in the front pocket of Josh''s apron.","category":"physical","source":"Apron find","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Lined paper, like a ledger page. Or like a lot of things.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Pantry door book","description":"Door book: the pantry delivery door opened four times between half past nine and ten; two collections signed out with folders, each with a scrawl in the name column that matches nobody on the staff list.","category":"record","source":"Door book","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Two folders left the pantry in the window with visitors. Either could hold a thin book.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Head baker on the cooling room","description":"Head baker''s statement: Josh went into the cooling room at about twenty past nine; she passed him a block of butter through the door at half past on her way back from the pantry, and did not see him again until the shelf was found bare; the judge walked the benches at her side from half past nine until then.","category":"testimony","source":"Head baker''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Forty minutes unseen in a room that shares a wall and a door with the pantry, while the judge, on her account, never left her side.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Pantry floor plan and the bolted door","description":"Pantry plan, annotated after the January inspection: the ledger shelf stands inside the delivery door, out of sight of the ovens; the connecting door from the cooling room is marked \"bolted pantry side, keep shut\", and the bolt was found home on Saturday.","category":"record","source":"Pantry plan","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The shelf is on the delivery side, where the folders wait, and the short way from the cooling room was bolted against him.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Flour rep at the cooling room window","description":"Flour rep''s statement: he arrived at twenty-five past nine for his folder, was told to wait, and stood at the cooling room window until ten watching the apprentice pipe a border round a cake, never once leaving the table; the head baker handed a butter block through the door while he watched.","category":"testimony","source":"Flour rep''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"A visitor with nothing else to look at kept Josh at the icing table through the window, and the butter came to him.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Practice recipe card in the cake box","description":"Cake box search: a lined recipe card with one corner torn off, \"Mum and Dad, thirty years\" written across the top, under the lid with an iced cake.","category":"physical","source":"Cake box search","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The scrap in his apron is this card''s corner, and the box holds a cake.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Stock camera at twenty to ten","description":"Stock camera, 09:41: the visiting judge, Mr Dray, alone in the pantry while the head baker has her back to it at the ovens, lifts the red-spined ledger from the shelf and slides it into the delivery folder open on the crate beside him; 09:55, the judge at the door with that folder, fat at the spine. The entry list pinned by the camera has entrant seven, C. Dray, with \"uncle\" on the contact line. Shown the still, the head baker allows that she turned to the ovens for the proving check at about twenty to ten and took the judge to be still behind her.","category":"record","source":"Stock camera","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The judge took the ledger into a folder and left with it, and his nephew is an entrant. The head baker had her back turned for the minutes that matter.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-6","title":"Head baker on the covered box","description":"Head baker''s statement: Josh put himself between her and a covered cake box in the cooling room and would not let her lift the lid.","category":"red_herring","source":"Head baker''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The box holds a surprise anniversary cake for his parents.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Where does the ledger live, and who goes past it?","category":"access","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Where were you between half past nine and ten?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What''s in that ledger worth taking?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-6"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"What''s in the covered box you won''t let anyone open?","category":"evidence","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"What''s the torn bit of paper in your apron from?","category":"evidence","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Why is there a scratched-out pantry line on your sheet?","category":"timeline","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Did you hear anything from the pantry while you were next door?","category":"timeline","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"So what is the paper in your pocket, if the box is a cake?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-5"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-9","text":"Who else was near that pantry while you were icing?","category":"access","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":["record-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-4","why":"Two folders left the pantry in the window, and the ledger is thin enough to ride in either."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The flour rep kept Josh at the icing table for every minute that matters, butter delivered to him."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The apron scrap is his own recipe card, and the box he guarded holds a cake."},{"evidenceId":"record-4","why":"The judge lifted the ledger into a folder and left with it, and his nephew is on the entry list."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-6","why":"The guarded box holds a surprise cake, not a ledger."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Where were you at twenty to ten?","Can anyone put you somewhere during the walk-round?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He was in the cooling room, which the flour rep confirms."},{"questionId":"question-4","alternativePhrasings":["Why would you keep the head baker away from a cake box?","What were you really doing in the cooling room?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-5"],"why":"He admits the surprise cake, which the search of the box bears out."},{"questionId":"question-8","alternativePhrasings":["Is the paper in your apron from the ledger?","Where did that torn corner come from?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The scrap is his recipe card''s corner, found in the cake box."},{"questionId":"question-7","alternativePhrasings":["Did anyone go into the pantry while you were icing?","When was the judge''s stock check?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"The judge was in the pantry at twenty to ten, and the camera has him lifting the ledger."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='2f6df772ae98799182f5704373d740d6bbd8422615610146edccbe9643231975');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"d5c63853-b8b0-42b0-a925-532b931a0ff5","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Going, Going","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Stoneport community radio station","crime":"The winning phone bid for the lighthouse lamp at the station''s on-air charity auction was written on a slip with no call behind it, in a name nobody can trace.","difficulty":"hard","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"fb969cd5bc96bc29e717d285e81a750b08477ef52f1bd7c78aa49ae3ccc6710b","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Stoneport Community Radio ran its spring charity auction live on Saturday night, with phone bids taken in the back room on three lines and web bids printing off the station''s form. The top lot was the old lighthouse lamp. At the close, nine o''clock, the presenter read out a winning phone bid of eight hundred in the name of a listener called R. Castle. The number on the slip belongs to a shop that shut last year, and nobody of that name has come forward. The slip is from the pad for line two, where Dee Marlow was rostered from eight until the close. The total read out on air cleared the five thousand a local benefactor had promised to match, by twenty. The station manager wants the slip explained before the benefactor is told anything, and Dee has been asked to stay behind in the green room.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Dee Marlow","ageRange":"thirties","role":"phone-room volunteer","avatarDescription":"Station polo shirt, headset pushed down round her neck, a pen behind one ear.","speechStyle":"Quick and chatty, laughs in the wrong places, apologises when she interrupts.","attitude":"Keen to be useful, and proud of the night''s total.","relationship":"Two years on the phones; put in for the station''s first paid assistant post, which the manager fills next month. Her father kept the lighthouse the lamp came out of."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"manager","actualEvent":"The station manager sent Dee through to the studio to read the running total, sat down at line two, wrote a slip for eight hundred in an invented name on the line two pad, and carried it to the presenter at two minutes to nine.","motive":"The benefactor''s match pledge paid only when the on-air total passed five thousand, and the manager had promised the trustees it would.","opportunity":"With Dee in the studio, the manager had the phone room to herself from ten to nine until the close.","means":"A slip from the line two pad in block capitals, written with the line silent and a number copied from an old listener list.","realAlibi":"I was on line two till about ten to nine, then the manager had me in the studio at the guest microphone till just after the close.","explanation":"The switchboard shows line two silent from ten to nine until after the close, so the slip was written without a call. The studio desk''s fader log has the guest microphone open from six minutes to nine until the close, and the presenter''s running order has Dee''s name against it, two rooms from the pad. The presenter had the slip from the manager''s hand, with the words that line two had just taken it. The three hundred and eighty web bid was Dee''s own, in her initials, for a lamp her father kept lit, and it broke a volunteer rule and nothing else. The caller after the close was the man she had outbid, checking the result from home.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-07T20:00:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T20:52:00Z","location":"phone room","description":"Dee takes bids on line two and fills a slip for each call.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-07T20:53:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T21:01:00Z","location":"studio","description":"Dee reads the running total from the pledge board at the guest microphone.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"manager","at":"2026-03-07T20:53:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T20:57:00Z","location":"phone room","description":"The manager sits at line two and writes the eight hundred slip on Dee''s pad.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"manager","at":"2026-03-07T20:58:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T20:59:00Z","location":"studio corridor","description":"The manager hands the slip to the presenter at the studio door.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-03-07T21:30:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T21:31:00Z","location":"green room","description":"Dee sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"phone room","from":"2026-03-07T19:30:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T22:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"manager","location":"phone room","from":"2026-03-07T19:00:00Z","until":"2026-03-07T22:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The manager sent me through to the studio at about ten to nine to read the pledge board on air.","disclosurePhrases":["The manager sent me through to the studio.","I was reading the pledge board on air.","I went through to read the board at ten to nine.","They wanted a voice on the board for the close."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"When I left the phone room the manager sat down at line two with my pad in front of her.","disclosurePhrases":["The manager took my chair at line two.","She was sitting at my line when I left.","My pad was in front of the manager.","The manager had line two after I went through."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-1"]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The three hundred and eighty web bid was mine, put in from the office under my initials, because my dad kept that light.","disclosurePhrases":["That three eighty bid was mine.","I put the web bid in myself.","I bid on the lamp for my dad.","My dad kept that light for years."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"The match pledge only paid out once the on-air total passed five thousand.","disclosurePhrases":["The pledge needed five thousand on air.","Under five thousand and the match was gone.","The benefactor doubles it past five thousand.","Five thousand was the line for the match."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The manager warned the whole room before the show that the match pledge would make or break the year.","disclosurePhrases":["The manager was sweating the total all night.","She kept checking the board every few minutes.","The match pledge was make or break for her.","She had promised the trustees the match."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["Who wrote the slip.","Whose number is on it.","What the manager did with the pad after she left the room."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-4"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-3","realFact":"The three hundred and eighty web bid was mine, put in from the office under my initials, because my dad kept that light.","permittedDeception":"I may suggest the web bid came from a listener on the lobby wifi, until the detective puts the initials or my father to me."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Winning slip from the line two pad","description":"Bid slip, line two pad, time box 20:57: \"lighthouse lamp, eight hundred, R. Castle\", block capitals, the number box filled with a line that belongs to a shop that shut last year.","category":"record","source":"Bid slip","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Written on her pad, in her window, in capitals that match none of her other slips. Either she wrote it or someone wrote it in her seat.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Switchboard log for line two","description":"Switchboard log: line two, last call connected 20:48:12 and cleared 20:49:40; next call 21:03:05. Lines one and three carried calls through to 21:00.","category":"record","source":"Switchboard log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The slip has a time with no call behind it. Whoever wrote it sat in the phone room with a silent line.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Web bid export for the lamp","description":"Bid form export: 20:31:22, lighthouse lamp, three hundred and eighty, name \"D. M.\", submitted from the station''s own office network.","category":"record","source":"Bid form export","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Someone inside the building bid on the lamp under initials, against the volunteer rule. The eight hundred slip lifted the lot past it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Engineer on the voice in the phone room","description":"Engineer''s statement: passing the phone room door at about five to nine he heard one voice, a woman''s, say \"eight hundred, lovely\" and the scratch of a pen, and kept walking. The station rota pinned on the phone room door has the manager in the studio with the presenter from ten to nine for the close.","category":"testimony","source":"Engineer''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"A woman spoke a bid aloud in the phone room with no call to speak to. Two women had been in that room that evening, and the rota puts one of them somewhere else.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Studio desk fader log and running order","description":"Studio desk fader log: guest microphone opened 20:54:10, closed 21:00:40, no gaps; the presenter''s running order for the close, marked up live in his hand, reads \"20:54 Dee on guest mic, board reads every half minute, 21:00 thank Dee by name\".","category":"record","source":"Studio desk","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The desk and the presenter''s own sheet keep her at the guest microphone through the whole window the slip could have been written in.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Presenter on where the slip came from","description":"Presenter''s statement: the manager came through from the phone room at two minutes to nine with the slip in her hand, said line two had just taken it, and he read it at the close; the manager was in the studio for none of the close, whatever the rota planned.","category":"testimony","source":"Presenter''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The slip reached the studio in the manager''s hand, from a room Dee had left, and the rota was a plan she did not keep.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Benefactor''s pledge letter and the board","description":"Pledge file: the benefactor''s letter doubles the auction total only when the on-air close exceeds five thousand; the manager''s own board photo at 20:56 shows four thousand six hundred, with the lamp at three hundred and eighty.","category":"record","source":"Pledge file","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Short with the lamp at three eighty; twenty over with it at eight hundred. The slip was sized to the pledge.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4","fact-5"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Confirmation on Dee''s phone","description":"Bid form confirmation on Dee''s phone, 20:31: three hundred and eighty, name \"D. M.\", note field \"for Dad, he kept that light going\".","category":"record","source":"Phone screen","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The web bid was hers, under her own name, and it breaks a volunteer rule and nothing else.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Presenter on the caller after the close","description":"Presenter''s statement: the studio line rang after nine and again a minute later, a man asking whether the lamp was still open and sounding put out when told it had gone.","category":"red_herring","source":"Presenter''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He had been outbid at twenty past eight and was checking from his home line. He touched no slip.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Who wrote the eight hundred slip on your pad?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Was the three hundred and eighty web bid yours?","category":"motive","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What did the match pledge hang on?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Who was sweating the number most?","category":"motive","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Walk me through your last ten minutes before the close.","category":"alibi","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Who had your chair after you left the line?","category":"access","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"How did that slip get to the presenter while you were on air?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"The slip was written in the phone room with the line silent, in the minutes the desk log has Dee two rooms away."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The fader log and the presenter''s running order keep Dee at the guest microphone through the whole window, by name."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The slip came to the presenter in the manager''s hand, from the chair Dee had given up."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The caller was the man Dee had outbid, ringing from home after the close."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Where were you at three minutes to nine?","Why is there a bid on your pad with no call?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"She was sent to the studio, which unlocks the desk log."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["Who was in the phone room after you left?","Who had your pad?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"The manager took her chair, which unlocks the presenter''s account of the slip."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Did you bid on the lamp yourself?","Whose initials are D. M.?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"Her hidden bid turns out to be her own, open and harmless."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='fb969cd5bc96bc29e717d285e81a750b08477ef52f1bd7c78aa49ae3ccc6710b');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"fa840ed4-f38d-421e-a85e-9131aea9ce04","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Overwritten Ridge","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Skerrow mountain hut","crime":"A walker''s route in the hut logbook was changed from the south col to the north ridge after he was reported overdue, and the search spent an hour on the wrong side of the hill.","difficulty":"easy","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"2514ea6bfb4fcd38f741f4fe82a0f8e3926dba2fecd45be211a9231f2fe3c0cc","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Tam Ashby set out from Skerrow Hut at five past one on Saturday with a day sack and a sandwich, and was overdue by half past six. His partner rang the hut, the hut rang base, and the search went up the north ridge, because that is what the logbook said. He walked into the tarn car park on the south side at ten past eight, cold and fine. When the team photographed the logbook page at five past seven, the words north ridge sat heavier on the line than anything round them. The south col path has been closed since Tuesday after a rockfall. Hal Brook has kept the hut for fifteen years. He took the partner''s call, read the book and radioed base. A couple of day walkers were in the common room through the evening, and the book sat on the table where it always sits. Hal is by the stove with the book on his knee.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Hal Brook","ageRange":"fifties","role":"hut warden","avatarDescription":"Wool jumper worn through at one elbow, weathered hands, a radio clipped to his belt.","speechStyle":"Slow, drops the ends of words. Calls the hill \"she\".","attitude":"Steady and low, asks after the walker before anything else and keeps a thumb on the radio clip.","relationship":"Fifteen years as warden; lives in the back room of the hut the year round; the hut association reviews every warden after a search call-out."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"After the overdue call Hal opened the logbook on the common room table, wrote north ridge to Caldbeck over Tam''s south col to the tarn, typed the changed route into the hut tablet''s incident form, and radioed it to base as what the book said.","motive":"He had told Tam the closed col would go for a quick walker, with his initials beside the line, and a walker found on a closed path on his say-so would have cost him the hut and the room he lives in.","opportunity":"He was alone with the book and the tablet in the common room for five minutes between the partner''s call and his radio call.","means":"The hut pen, a line with room on it, and a tablet form he believed kept only the saved version.","realAlibi":"I took the call from his partner about twenty-five to seven, went straight to the book, read his line to base, and I never had a pen in my hand.","explanation":"Tam photographs his logbook entry every walk, and his picture at ten past one shows south col to the tarn with Hal''s initials beside it. The hut tablet keeps every edit to an incident form, and at sixteen minutes to seven Hal typed south col, cleared it, and saved north ridge a minute before radioing the ridge to base. Tam says Hal gave him the col at the door and watched him write it. The woman in the blue hat runs the Caldbeck bunkhouse on the north side and was writing her own party in for the night on the next line at a quarter past six; the col was still in Hal''s own form at sixteen minutes to seven, long after she had gone up.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T13:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T13:08:00Z","location":"hut door","description":"Hal talks Tam through the col at the door and initials the advised column beside the col.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T18:35:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T18:38:00Z","location":"warden''s room","description":"Hal takes the call from Tam''s partner.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T18:40:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T18:45:00Z","location":"common room","description":"Hal writes north ridge to Caldbeck over the route line and types the changed route into the tablet form.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T18:46:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T18:48:00Z","location":"radio desk","description":"Hal radios base with the north ridge route as read from the book.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T21:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T21:01:00Z","location":"common room","description":"Hal sits down by the stove for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"common room","from":"2026-04-18T06:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T23:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The south col path has been shut since Tuesday after the rockfall, and I put the notice up myself.","disclosurePhrases":["I put the closure notice up myself.","The col has been shut since Tuesday.","The rockfall closed the col on Tuesday.","That notice on the board is mine."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I talked Tam through the col at the door and initialled his line.","disclosurePhrases":["I gave him the col at the door.","We talked about the col on the step.","I initialled his line at the door.","The col came up when he was writing in."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"I typed his route into the tablet incident form before I radioed base.","disclosurePhrases":["I filled the tablet form first.","The incident form went in before the radio call.","I had the tablet open before I called base.","The form was done by a quarter to seven."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"A walker found on a closed path on my advice would have cost me the hut and the room I live in.","disclosurePhrases":["The association would have had me out for that.","My room goes with the job.","A closed path on my advice ends the warden.","They review every warden after a call-out."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The book lies on the common room table all evening and anyone passing can write in it.","disclosurePhrases":["The book lies on the table all evening.","Anyone in the room could write in it.","The pen lives in the spine of the book.","Folk write in that book all evening."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-6","statement":"I took the partner''s call in the back room at twenty-five to seven and went from the phone straight to the table.","disclosurePhrases":["I took the call in the back room.","I was on the phone to his partner at twenty-five to seven.","The call came through to the back room.","I went from the phone straight to the table."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"doesNotKnow":["That Tam photographs his entries.","That the tablet keeps edit history.","What the day walkers saw."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I talked Tam through the col at the door and initialled his line.","permittedDeception":"I may say Tam wrote north ridge himself after I warned him off the col, and that my initials went on after a word about the ridge, until his photo or his own account is put to me."},{"factId":"fact-3","realFact":"I typed his route into the tablet incident form before I radioed base.","permittedDeception":"I may say I radioed straight from the book and only did the tablet form later in the evening, until the form history is put to me."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Logbook page as photographed by the team","description":"Search team photo, 19:05: line for T. Ashby, out 13:05, route \"north ridge to Caldbeck\", the words north ridge pressed heavier into the page than the rest of the line; advised column initialled H.B.","category":"record","source":"Search team photo","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The route was rewritten, and the initials put Hal beside the line before it changed. Whether he wrote the second version is the case.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Base radio log for the evening","description":"Rescue base log: 18:46 Skerrow Hut, H. Brook, \"book has him north ridge to Caldbeck, out thirteen oh five\". 19:52 ridge team, no sign. 20:10 walker self-reports from the tarn car park.","category":"record","source":"Rescue base log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Hal gave base the ridge eleven minutes after the partner''s call. The search went where he pointed it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Closure notice on the path board","description":"Path board: south col route closed after rockfall, dated Tuesday, signed by the warden; a laminated map beneath it has the col route crossed through in marker.","category":"physical","source":"Path board","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Hal knew the col was shut and put it in writing. Advising it anyway would be the thing to hide.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Partner on the call to the hut","description":"Partner''s statement: Tam told her at breakfast he fancied the tarn; when she rang the hut at twenty-five to seven the man who answered said he would check the book and ring base.","category":"testimony","source":"Partner''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Tam meant the tarn in the morning. The book said the ridge by evening. Somebody changed a plan, or a line.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Tam''s photo of his entry","description":"Walker''s phone, 13:10: a photo of his logbook line, route \"south col to the tarn\", advised column initialled H.B., the lines above it matching the team''s photo and the line below still blank.","category":"record","source":"Walker''s phone","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The route was the col when Hal initialled it. His initials sat beside the closed path, and the ridge came later.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Incident form history on the hut tablet","description":"Hut tablet, incident form edit history: 18:44:05 route field \"south col / tarn\" typed under the warden''s login; 18:44:50 field cleared; 18:45:20 saved as \"north ridge / Caldbeck\".","category":"record","source":"Hut tablet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"He typed the col first, then replaced it a minute before he radioed the ridge.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Tam on the word at the door","description":"Walker''s statement: Hal told him at the door the col would go for a quick walker and the rockfall was mostly cleared, and watched him write the col in the book.","category":"testimony","source":"Walker''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The advice was the col, from the man whose notice closed it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Association rule on advice","description":"Hut association handbook, wardens'' section: advising a walker onto a closed route is listed as grounds for immediate removal, with the warden''s room vacated within the month.","category":"record","source":"Hut association handbook","kind":"observation","interpretation":"What a walker found on the col with his initials beside it would have cost him.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-5","title":"Bunk book and the Caldbeck letter","description":"Hut bunk book: R. Penhale, party of two, in for the night, 18:16, her logbook entry on the line below Tam''s in blue ink; Penhale runs the Caldbeck bunkhouse on the north side and wrote to the association in March that Skerrow \"steers every walker south to the tarn and past my door\"; the day walkers put her on the bunk room stair by twenty past six, and the tablet form still had the col in it at 18:44.","category":"record","source":"Hut bunk book","kind":"observation","interpretation":"A woman with a reason to want walkers on the ridge was at the book, and the col was still on the line half an hour after she left it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Day walkers on the woman at the book","description":"Day walkers'' statement: at about a quarter past six a woman in a blue hat was writing in the logbook at the common room table and shut it when they came in; she went up to the bunk room after.","category":"red_herring","source":"Day walkers'' statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"She was writing her own party in for the night. Her entry is on the next line, and Hal''s own tablet still had the col in Tam''s line half an hour after she had gone up.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"What did you and Tam talk about at the door?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"What did you do between the partner''s call and the radio call?","category":"timeline","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What happens to you if a walker turns up on the col on your say-so?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Who else could get at that book tonight?","category":"access","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"What state is the col in, really?","category":"evidence","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Why does his photo show the col beside your initials?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Where did the col in the tablet form come from?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["record-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"Where were you when his partner rang?","category":"alibi","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-6"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The route line was rewritten, and Hal''s initials sit beside it from before the change."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"At ten past one the line said south col with his initials on it, against his account that Tam wrote the ridge."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"He typed the col into the form and replaced it with the ridge a minute before he radioed the ridge as what the book said."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-4","why":"The woman in the blue hat was writing her own party in, on the next line, and the col was still in Hal''s form half an hour after she left the table."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Did you advise him on his route?","Why are your initials on his line?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He gave Tam the col, which the walker''s photo and his statement confirm."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Did you use the tablet before you radioed?","What did you do before you called base?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The form went in first, and its history has the col in it."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["What would the association do to you?","What were you afraid of?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"The hut and the room were on the line."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='2514ea6bfb4fcd38f741f4fe82a0f8e3926dba2fecd45be211a9231f2fe3c0cc');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"8528f0ac-1567-461e-ab22-0c1151209611","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Thirty Seconds a Move","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Chapel Street library hall, town chess final","crime":"During the break in the town chess final the clock was reset so the challenger''s side gained no increment, and he lost on time in a winning position.","difficulty":"medium","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"3ad94aaca060efb09a23db4f37057f5b45859728bf6991e2b9cff6e437ccce33","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"The town championship final was played on Saturday afternoon in the Chapel Street library hall, with a camera over the board and a live stream for the forty people who could not fit in. Mo Lindqvist, the champion, had white. Russell Pike, the challenger, had black. Each side gets ninety minutes and thirty seconds a move. At ten past three the arbiter paused the clock for the agreed break, and play restarted at twenty past. Russell lost on time at two minutes past four in a position the stream''s engine called winning for him. The clock, examined afterwards, has white''s increment at thirty seconds and black''s at none. The club''s saved presets set both sides alike. Gerald Vance was assistant arbiter and checked the clocks before every round, as he has for thirty years. The champion and his son were both near the table during the break. Gerald is waiting in the reading room with the clock in its box.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Gerald Vance","ageRange":"sixties","role":"assistant arbiter","avatarDescription":"Tweed jacket, club tie, reading glasses on a cord, a pencil stub in the top pocket.","speechStyle":"Precise and courtly, fond of the phrase \"as it were\". Explains the rule before answering the question.","attitude":"Rattled and over-polite, apologises for repeating himself and asks twice whether the challenger is still in the building.","relationship":"Thirty years in the club and the champion''s Thursday opponent for twenty of them; the challenger''s motion last spring replaced the club clocks and folded Gerald''s equipment post into the secretary''s."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"During the break Gerald picked up the paused clock, held the centre button into the settings menu, set black''s increment to zero with white''s unchanged, and set it back square on the table before anyone returned.","motive":"He wanted Mo to take the cup in his last final before moving away, and he blamed Russell for the motion that ended his equipment post.","opportunity":"The clock sat paused and unattended on the table for the first seven minutes of the break while the players and the arbiter were out of the hall.","means":"The clock''s settings menu, which he knows better than anyone in the club, reached by holding the centre button.","realAlibi":"I straightened the clock square to the board during the break, as I do, and I glanced at the paused display. I never went near the menu.","explanation":"The stream recording shows Gerald''s hands on the clock for twenty-five seconds at twelve minutes past three, the centre button held and a run of presses, with the settings screen visible on the display; the broadcast log shows black gaining thirty seconds a move until the break and nothing after it. The champion turned the clock to read the display for four seconds. His son, who had the cup engraved for his father on Friday, adjusted the camera arm and touched nothing on the table. Gerald''s message to the club chat the night before names the increment as the thing that beats Mo.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-16T14:00:00Z","until":"2026-05-16T15:09:00Z","location":"arbiter''s table","description":"Gerald keeps the score sheets and watches the final from the arbiter''s table.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-16T15:11:00Z","until":"2026-05-16T15:15:00Z","location":"final board","description":"Gerald picks up the paused clock, enters the settings menu and sets black''s increment to zero.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-16T15:16:00Z","until":"2026-05-16T16:05:00Z","location":"arbiter''s table","description":"Gerald returns to the arbiter''s table for the restart and the end of the game.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"champion","at":"2026-05-16T15:17:00Z","until":"2026-05-16T15:18:00Z","location":"final board","description":"Mo comes back early and turns the clock to read the paused display.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-16T17:30:00Z","until":"2026-05-16T17:31:00Z","location":"reading room","description":"Gerald sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"final board","from":"2026-05-16T13:00:00Z","until":"2026-05-16T17:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"Both club presets set the two sides the same, so a one-sided increment has to be put in by hand.","disclosurePhrases":["The presets set both sides alike.","A one-sided increment has to be keyed in by hand.","You can only get that by hand.","Somebody went into the menu for that."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I picked the clock up during the break and held the centre button to check the display.","disclosurePhrases":["I picked the clock up at the break.","I held the centre button to read the display.","The clock was in my hands for a moment.","I checked the display with the clock in hand."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"Russell plays fast and lives on the increment, and I have watched him do it all season.","disclosurePhrases":["Russell lives on the increment.","He plays the whole game on the thirty seconds.","Take the increment away and he is lost.","I have watched him burn his clock all season."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"Mo is moving away next month and this was his last final, and I wanted him to have the cup.","disclosurePhrases":["I wanted Mo to have the cup.","This was his last final before he moves.","Twenty years of Thursdays with the man.","I wanted him to go out holding it."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"Russell''s motion in the spring took the equipment post I had held for twenty years.","disclosurePhrases":["His motion cost me the equipment post.","The clocks were my job until the spring.","Russell had my post folded into the secretary''s.","Twenty years of looking after the clocks, gone."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-6","statement":"I stayed in the hall through the break while the chief arbiter and the players went out.","disclosurePhrases":["I stayed in the hall through the break.","I kept the hall while the others went out.","I was in the hall the whole break.","The chief arbiter went out and I stayed."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"doesNotKnow":["That the stream was recording through the break.","What the broadcast log keeps per move.","What the spectators saw."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I picked the clock up during the break and held the centre button to check the display.","permittedDeception":"I may say I only squared the clock to the board where it sat and glanced at the paused display, without lifting it or touching a button, until the stream recording is put to me."},{"factId":"fact-4","realFact":"Mo is moving away next month and this was his last final, and I wanted him to have the cup.","permittedDeception":"I may say an arbiter has no preference between players, until the detective presses on my Thursday games with Mo or the club chat."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Clock settings after the game","description":"Clock examination: white ninety minutes with thirty seconds a move; black ninety minutes with no increment; the club''s two saved presets both set the sides alike; the change is a manual entry in the settings menu.","category":"record","source":"Clock examination","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Somebody who knew the menu made a one-sided change by hand. The question is who had the clock long enough.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Arbiter''s sheet for the final","description":"Arbiter''s sheet: clock paused 15:10, restarted 15:21; margin note in the assistant arbiter''s hand, \"15:12 clocks checked, paused, G.V.\", as on every round of the day; the chief arbiter and both players out of the hall from the pause until 15:17, the assistant keeping the sheet at the table.","category":"record","source":"Arbiter''s sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Gerald''s initials sit on the break as on every round, with a time. The hall was his for seven minutes, and the champion''s for the four after.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Challenger on his clock","description":"Challenger''s statement: somewhere around move forty his time felt short for the moves he had made; he played on, flagged at 16:02, and then asked to see the settings; he moved the spring motion that replaced the club clocks.","category":"testimony","source":"Challenger''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He noticed late and touched nothing. His motion is the thing Gerald lost by.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Stream recording over the break","description":"Stream recording, 15:12:40 to 15:13:05: Gerald''s hands on the clock, the centre button held, then a run of presses; the settings screen is readable on the display before he sets it down square to the board.","category":"record","source":"Stream recording","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Against his account of squaring the clock where it sat, he had it up and in the menu for the length of time the change takes.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Broadcast move log","description":"Board broadcast log: black''s clock gains thirty seconds after every move up to move twenty-eight; from move twenty-nine, the first after the 15:21 restart, no increment is added on any black move; white''s increment runs throughout.","category":"record","source":"Board broadcast","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The change happened in the break and only on black''s side.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Club chat message from Friday night","description":"Club group chat, Friday 22:14, from Gerald: \"Russell lives on the increment. Take that away and Mo walks it.\"","category":"testimony","source":"Club group chat","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He named the exact thing that was taken away, the night before.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Spring general meeting minutes","description":"Club minutes, spring general meeting: the challenger''s motion to replace the clocks carried; the equipment officer post, held by G. Vance, folded into the secretary''s role.","category":"record","source":"Club minutes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The grievance is on paper, with the challenger''s name on the motion.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}},{"id":"record-5","title":"Trophy shop receipt and the son at the table","description":"Club file: a trophy shop receipt dated Friday, \"engrave cup, M. Lindqvist, town champion\", paid by the champion''s son, for last year''s name, which the holder has cut before the cup comes back for the final; stream recording 15:17:30 to 15:18:10, the son''s hands on the camera arm only, the clock face untouched, his father''s four seconds with the clock in frame beside him.","category":"record","source":"Club file","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The son paid to have last year''s name put on the cup before it was handed back, as the holder must, and the recording he was adjusting has his hands nowhere near the clock.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-6"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Spectator on the champion and the clock","description":"Spectator''s statement: the champion came back from the break ahead of the others and had both hands on the clock, turning it round to face him.","category":"red_herring","source":"Spectator''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The stream has him reading the display for four seconds and setting it down. He pressed nothing.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Spectator on the son at the table","description":"Spectator''s statement: the champion''s son, who runs the stream laptop, was leaning over the final board during the break with the camera arm in one hand.","category":"red_herring","source":"Spectator''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He was re-aiming the camera. The recording he was adjusting is the one that shows his father touching nothing and Gerald in the menu.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"What did you do with the clock during the break?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"How does a clock end up with one side on an increment and the other without?","category":"evidence","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"Who did you want to win?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"How did you take it when the new clocks came in?","category":"motive","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Does Russell manage his clock well?","category":"timeline","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"What were you setting while you held the centre button?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["record-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Who else knew the increment was his whole game?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-3"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"Where were you while the players were out of the hall?","category":"alibi","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-6"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The change was manual and one-sided, by someone who knew the menu."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The stream has him in the settings menu for twenty-five seconds, against his account of squaring the clock where it sat."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"Black''s increment stops at the restart and only on black''s side, which places the change in the break he had alone."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-2","why":"The champion turned the clock to read it for four seconds and pressed nothing."},{"evidenceId":"exhibit-5","why":"The son was re-aiming the camera that recorded the break."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Did you touch the clock during the break?","What did you check on the clock at ten past three?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He claims squaring only, then concedes picking it up and holding the button when the stream is put to him."},{"questionId":"question-5","alternativePhrasings":["How does Russell use his time?","Does the increment matter to Russell?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"He knows the challenger lives on the increment, which unlocks the move log."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["Were you rooting for Mo?","Do you have a favourite between them?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"He wanted Mo to take the cup, and the chat message says so the night before."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='3ad94aaca060efb09a23db4f37057f5b45859728bf6991e2b9cff6e437ccce33');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"87a6bc35-a72d-487e-a9e4-785bc7c8c88a","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Results Final","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Larkhill school hall, science fair night","crime":"The judges'' handwritten results sheet was replaced in its envelope by a typed one with first and second place swapped before the head teacher announced the winners.","difficulty":"hard","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"fa8ca9a91b57b071956e6793c61c318b5ed5601426e3d4f826b86f8dc794f25b","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Larkhill''s science fair night ended on Thursday with the head teacher opening the judges'' envelope on stage and reading out a sheet none of the judges had written. The head judge, a retired engineer who scored with two parent volunteers, totalled the marks by hand, signed the sheet, closed it in the envelope in the office at five to seven and left it on the desk. What came out at half past was typed, with his name typed where the signature had been, and the honey and mould project in first place over the paper plane. He had photographed the original. First place carries the school shield and a place at the regional fair. The office printer logged the typed sheet at seven minutes past, under the staff account of Nell Harker, who runs the fair and teaches the form the honey project comes from. The office stays open through the evening for the raffle. Nell has a stall to pack away and has given you until the caretaker locks up. Was it her, and what carries it?","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Nell Harker","ageRange":"forties","role":"science teacher","avatarDescription":"Lab coat over a cardigan, a lanyard of keys, bicarbonate dust on both cuffs.","speechStyle":"Fast and clipped, talks in numbered lists, and says \"right\" once she has decided something.","attitude":"Brisk and amused, treats the questions as a quiz she expects to win, and checks the clock on the wall between answers.","relationship":"Nine years at the school; her form has lost the science shield three years running, and she argued with the head judge about the scoring rubric all week."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"pta-chair","actualEvent":"The PTA chair, alone in the office to collect the raffle float, found the staff computer still signed in, opened the results template from the fair folder, typed in the marks from the sheet in the tucked envelope with the top two swapped, printed it, and put it in the envelope in place of the signed one.","motive":"His son''s honey and mould project had placed second by two marks, and the regional place had been promised to the boy at home.","opportunity":"He had the office to himself from four minutes past seven to nine minutes past, with the envelope on the desk and the computer awake.","means":"Nell''s open session on the office computer, the office printer, and an envelope closed with a tuck of the flap.","realAlibi":"I was on the hall stage with the volcano from about two minutes to seven until twenty past, in front of a hundred parents and the stream camera.","explanation":"The parents'' stream has Nell on the stage without a break from two minutes to seven until twenty past, and the office is across the yard. The office computer shows her session was never signed out after the certificates at twenty to seven, and the results file was created and printed inside it while she was on stage. The float book has the PTA chair in the office from four minutes past seven to nine minutes past, which covers the print, and the safe keypad has his code at five past and eight past. The fair entry list has his son against the honey and mould project. She asked about the honey project at the office door because the pupil''s mother was leaving for a night shift and wanted to know. The co-judge in the corridor was carrying the scoring cards to the hall board, and the stream has her pinning them up at six minutes past.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-11T18:38:00Z","until":"2026-06-11T18:43:00Z","location":"office","description":"Nell prints the certificates on the office computer and leaves without signing out.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-11T18:54:00Z","until":"2026-06-11T18:56:00Z","location":"office doorway","description":"Nell asks the head judge at the door whether the honey project placed.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-11T18:58:00Z","until":"2026-06-11T19:22:00Z","location":"hall stage","description":"Nell runs the volcano demonstration on stage, on the parents'' stream.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"pta-chair","at":"2026-06-11T19:04:00Z","until":"2026-06-11T19:09:00Z","location":"office","description":"The PTA chair signs in for the float, types and prints the swapped sheet in Nell''s open session, switches the envelope''s contents, and signs out.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-06-11T19:45:00Z","until":"2026-06-11T19:46:00Z","location":"prep room","description":"Nell sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"pta-chair","location":"office","from":"2026-06-11T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-06-11T21:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"I was on the hall stage with the volcano from just before seven until twenty past.","disclosurePhrases":["I was on stage with the volcano.","The volcano ran from just before seven.","A hundred parents watched me do the volcano.","I was up on the stage until twenty past."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I printed the certificates on the office computer at twenty to seven and walked off without signing out.","disclosurePhrases":["I left myself signed in on the office computer.","I printed the certificates and walked off.","My login was still open on that machine.","I forgot to sign out after the certificates."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The office computer never locks itself, so anyone who sat at it while my session was open could print as me.","disclosurePhrases":["That machine stays open to anyone who sits down.","Anyone at that desk could print as me.","The office computer stays awake for whoever sits at it.","Whoever sat down could print under my name."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I asked about the honey project at the office door because the pupil''s mother was leaving for a night shift and wanted to know.","disclosurePhrases":["Her mum was leaving for a night shift.","The mother wanted to know before her shift.","I was asking for the pupil''s mum.","She had to leave before the announcement."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The honey project is from my form, and the shield goes to the winner''s form.","disclosurePhrases":["The honey project is one of mine.","The shield goes with first place.","That project came out of my form.","My form would get the shield with it."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["Who sat at the office computer after her.","What was in the envelope when the head judge left it.","Why the PTA chair was in the office so long.","Whose child the honey project belongs to beyond her form list."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-5"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4"],"misleading":[]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Office printer log","description":"Printer log, Thursday: 18:40:52, certificates.docx, n.harker, twenty-four pages; 19:07:15, results_final.docx, n.harker, one page; nothing else after six.","category":"record","source":"Printer log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The typed sheet came out under her account. An account is a login, and a login is a person only while that person is sitting at it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"The two sheets","description":"Envelope contents: a typed sheet, head judge''s name typed on the signature line, honey and mould first, paper plane second; the head judge''s phone photo at 18:54 shows the handwritten original, signed, paper plane first by two marks.","category":"physical","source":"Envelope contents","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Whoever typed it knew the marks well enough to swap the top two and leave the rest alone, and had a printer within reach.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Head judge on the office door","description":"Head judge''s statement: he tucked the envelope shut and left it on the office desk at five to seven; Nell met him at the door on his way out and asked whether the honey project had placed, and he said she would hear with everyone else.","category":"testimony","source":"Head judge''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"She wanted the result before the envelope was opened, and was refused it. There is a reason for that in the file and a reason outside it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Rubric email from Monday","description":"School email, Monday, Nell to the head judge: \"if presentation outweighs method again you will hand it to a paper plane, and I will say so from the stage.\"","category":"record","source":"School email","kind":"observation","interpretation":"She had told him in writing which project she thought should lose. The swap is the result she wanted.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Parents'' stream recording","description":"Parents'' stream, 18:58:30 to 19:22:10: Nell on the hall stage with the volcano and a volunteer holding the tray, in frame without a break; the office is across the yard from the hall.","category":"record","source":"Parents'' stream","kind":"observation","interpretation":"She was on stage when the sheet was typed and printed, with a yard between her and the office.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Office computer session log","description":"Office computer event log: n.harker signed in 18:38:10, no sign-out; screen woke 19:04:30; results_final.docx saved 19:05:10 as a copy of results_template.docx from the fair folder, the twelve project names already in it; twelve mark boxes and a name line typed; printed 19:07:15 from the same session.","category":"record","source":"Office computer","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The account was open to whoever sat down. Two minutes with the template and the marks from the tucked envelope made the sheet, while she was on stage.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Raffle float book and safe keypad","description":"Office float book, in the PTA chair''s hand: \"19:04 in for float, office empty\" and \"19:09 out, locked behind me\"; the safe keypad log has his code at 19:05 to open and 19:08 to relock; the head judge''s envelope lay on the desk beside the book. The fair entry list clipped inside the book''s cover gives the honey and mould project''s entrant the chair''s surname.","category":"record","source":"Office float book","kind":"observation","interpretation":"One adult alone in the office with the envelope and the open computer from four minutes past to nine past, which covers the print, and the project that moved up is his son''s.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Pupil''s mother on the night shift","description":"Parent''s statement: the mother of the honey project''s pupil had to leave at seven for a night shift and asked Nell to find out whether her daughter had placed; Nell came back with \"they are making us wait\".","category":"testimony","source":"Parent''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The question at the door was a favour, and the answer she got was none.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-5","title":"Scoring cards on the hall board","description":"Parents'' stream, 19:06:20, edge of frame: the parent judge pinning the judges'' scoring cards to the hall display board, a sheaf of them in her hand; the hall steward''s note has her taking the cards from the stock shelf outside the office at 19:03.","category":"record","source":"Parents'' stream","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The co-judge in the corridor was carrying scoring cards to the hall, and was on the stream pinning them up while the sheet printed.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Hall steward on the co-judge","description":"Hall steward''s statement: one of the parent judges was in the corridor outside the office at about five past seven with a sheaf of papers, and hurried off toward the hall when he greeted her.","category":"red_herring","source":"Hall steward''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The sheaf was the judges'' scoring cards for the display board; the stream has her pinning them up in the hall at six minutes past.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Walk me through your evening from seven o''clock.","category":"alibi","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"How did that sheet come out under your login?","category":"evidence","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"Why ask the head judge about the honey project at the door?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Could anyone else have printed under your login?","category":"access","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"What does first place do for you?","category":"motive","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Who could have sat at that machine while it was awake?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["record-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The sheet was printed from her account at seven minutes past, a time the stream has her on stage."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The stream keeps her on the stage without a break through the minutes the sheet was made."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"Her session was left open, and the file was created at the keyboard while she was on stage."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The PTA chair was alone in the office with the envelope and the open computer across the minutes of the print, and the project that moved up is his son''s."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-5","why":"The co-judge was carrying the scoring cards to the hall board, and the stream has her pinning them up."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Where were you when the sheet was printed?","What were you doing at seven?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"She was on stage, which unlocks the stream recording."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Did you sign out of the office computer?","Who else can print under your account?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She left her session open, which unlocks the computer log."},{"questionId":"question-4","alternativePhrasings":["Does the office computer lock itself?","Could someone else print as you?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The machine stays open to whoever sits down, which unlocks the float book and the name of who did."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='fa8ca9a91b57b071956e6793c61c318b5ed5601426e3d4f826b86f8dc794f25b');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"2d8c6397-240a-49f8-ae0a-0944ceadab54","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Rule Six","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Hollin Bank allotment society show","crime":"The favourite in the heaviest marrow class was found wiped with linseed oil on the morning of judging and disqualified under show rules.","difficulty":"easy","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"198b686da6121e5f1bf17c0dcf9d59555ca5c763a3dd4f077efde81d727c9bdb","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"At twenty to eight on Saturday, with judging due at nine, the duty steward unzipped the Hollin Bank marquee and the smell of linseed met her at the door. Ron Tapley''s marrow, the favourite for the shield, was glossy from stalk to tip with an oil ring beneath it, and the judge disqualified it on the spot under the rule against polishing. Ron told the judge the only cloth he used was dry. The marquee was tied shut at nine on Friday by Gwen Pardoe, the set-up steward, whose own marrow was the second favourite. Four stewards share the box of ties and cloths, and Gwen holds the key to the shed where the society keeps linseed for the trestle legs. A plot-holder saw a van at the field gate at half past six and somebody walking from it through the hedge gap. Gwen signed in at the gate two minutes before the briefing, has applied for the same double plot as Ron, and keeps a headtorch in her car. The judge wants the class weighed by ten.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Gwen Pardoe","ageRange":"early sixties","role":"show steward and plot-holder","avatarDescription":"Green society gilet over a checked shirt, a tape measure clipped to the pocket, boots still wet from the field.","speechStyle":"Clipped. Calls her marrow \"the beast\" and the show \"the circus\".","attitude":"Flat and watchful, arms folded, answers the question asked and stops.","relationship":"Has stewarded the show for nine years and grown marrows for twelve. The shield winner gets a year''s rent free and first call on the double plot by the trough, which she and Ron have each applied for."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"Just before six on Saturday Gwen came through the hedge gap with a headtorch, cut the white ties, wiped Ron''s marrow over with boiled linseed on a square of the stewards'' blue cloth, dropped the cloth in the marquee bin and tied the flap shut again with blue ties from the stewards'' box.","motive":"The shield carries a year''s rent free and first call on the double plot, and she had come second to Ron three years running with a marrow she thought was her best yet.","opportunity":"As set-up steward she held the box of ties and cloths and knew the hedge gap avoided the gate.","means":"Boiled linseed from her shed, a square of the stewards'' blue cloth, and fresh ties to close the flap behind her.","realAlibi":"I tied the marquee shut at nine on Friday, went home, and came down at eight on Saturday for the stewards'' briefing.","explanation":"Boiled linseed on marrow skin goes tacky within the hour and the smell fills a tent, so an oiling at six reads as a wet gloss and a stink by twenty to eight. The trail camera on the hedge has a figure in a society gilet going into the marquee at eight minutes to six and out at nine minutes past, against her claim that she stayed away until eight, and the gate book has her coming in at two minutes to eight because the hedge gap avoids the gate. The cloth in the bin is cut from the blue roll signed out to her, and the blue ties on the flap came from the same box, which was signed out to her from Friday night until the briefing, so no other steward had ties or cloth at six; the camera figure carries that box. Wilf Bray has a marrow and a plot application of his own, and no box. The van at half past six was the milk delivery for the tea tent; the driver left a crate at the gap and drove off.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-08-14T19:00:00Z","until":"2026-08-14T21:00:00Z","location":"show marquee","description":"Gwen sets out the trestles, logs four white ties on the door flap and signs the marquee closed.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-08-15T05:48:00Z","until":"2026-08-15T06:10:00Z","location":"show marquee","description":"Gwen cuts the ties, wipes the marrow with linseed, bins the cloth and reties the flap in blue.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-08-15T06:15:00Z","until":"2026-08-15T07:50:00Z","location":"home","description":"Gwen drives home, showers and eats breakfast.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"milk-driver","at":"2026-08-15T06:28:00Z","until":"2026-08-15T06:34:00Z","location":"field gate","description":"The milk delivery leaves a crate at the hedge gap for the tea tent and drives off.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-08-15T07:58:00Z","until":"2026-08-15T08:20:00Z","location":"show tent","description":"Gwen signs in at the gate and arrives for the stewards'' briefing.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-6","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-08-15T09:30:00Z","until":"2026-08-15T09:31:00Z","location":"show tent","description":"Gwen sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"show marquee","from":"2026-08-14T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-08-14T22:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"suspect","location":"show marquee","from":"2026-08-15T05:00:00Z","until":"2026-08-15T12:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"Boiled linseed on marrow skin goes tacky within the hour and the smell fills a tent.","disclosurePhrases":["Linseed goes tacky on a marrow within the hour.","You can smell linseed across a tent.","A wiped marrow shines while the oil is still wet.","An hour of linseed and the whole tent reeks."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I was on the showground before six on Saturday morning.","disclosurePhrases":["I was down on the field before six.","I came through the hedge gap at first light.","I did go down early on Saturday.","I was on the showground before anyone."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The blue ties and the blue cloth roll were in the stewards'' box, and I signed the box out on Friday.","disclosurePhrases":["I signed the stewards box out on Friday.","The blue ties came out of my box.","That cloth roll was in the box with me.","I had the stewards box all weekend."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"The shield carries a year''s rent free and first call on the double plot, and I have been second to Ron three years running.","disclosurePhrases":["The shield comes with a year of free rent.","I have been second to Ron three years running.","The winner gets first call on the double plot.","Three years I have come second to that man."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"Wilf does the seven o''clock rope check from outside and leaves the flap tied.","disclosurePhrases":["Wilf does the ropes from the outside.","Wilf leaves the flap tied on his check.","The rope check is all done from outside.","Wilf walks round the outside at seven."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["That there is a trail camera on the hedge gap.","What the milk driver has said.","Whether the judge kept the cloth from the bin."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I was on the showground before six on Saturday morning.","permittedDeception":"I may say I tied it shut at nine on Friday and set foot on the field again only at two minutes to eight on Saturday, through the gate, until the trail camera is put to me."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Judge''s disqualification note","description":"Judge''s note, Saturday: duty steward unzipped the marquee at 07:40 and reported a smell of linseed; entry fourteen, R. Tapley, glossy from end to end and still tacky, with an oil ring on the trestle cloth; disqualified under rule six, \"oiling, waxing or polishing of exhibits\".","category":"record","source":"Judge''s note","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Someone oiled it after it went on the trestle and before the steward opened up. Rule six did the rest.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Marquee door ties","description":"Marquee inspection: the Friday set-up log records four white ties on the door flap at nine, the hire firm''s ties that come fitted to the tent; on Saturday morning the flap carried four blue ties, with the cut ends of white ones in the grass beneath; blue ties are the society''s own and live in the stewards'' box.","category":"physical","source":"Marquee inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Somebody cut their way in overnight and closed it again with ties from the stewards'' box.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Stewards'' rota and gate book","description":"Stewards'' rota: G. Pardoe set-up steward Friday, marquee signed closed at nine; W. Bray rope check Saturday seven; four stewards listed against the box of ties and cloths; briefing for all four at eight. Gate book, Saturday: W. Bray in 06:55; G. Pardoe in 07:58; the other two stewards in at 08:00 and 08:03.","category":"record","source":"Stewards'' rota","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Four people are entitled to that box. The gate book has all four arriving after the oil was on, and the gate is one way onto the field.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Grower on the dry cloth","description":"Grower''s statement: Ron Tapley wiped the marrow down with a dry tea towel on Friday afternoon before it went on the trestle, left the field at five, and came back at half past seven on Saturday with his wife.","category":"testimony","source":"Grower''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"A dry wipe is allowed. The gloss came later, from something other than a tea towel.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-6","title":"Society shed list","description":"Society shed list, checked Saturday: boiled linseed, one tin, for the trestle legs, lid loose and the level down; the shed key is held by the set-up steward for show week; the shed stands beside the hedge gap.","category":"record","source":"Society shed list","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The oil was to hand for whoever had the shed key, and the shed is by the gap, which is a second finger pointed at the same person.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Trail camera on the hedge gap","description":"Trail camera at the hedge gap, Saturday: 05:52 a figure in a society gilet with a headtorch and a flat box under one arm comes through the gap and into the marquee; 06:09 the same figure leaves by the gap; 06:31 the milk driver sets a crate down at the gap and walks back to the van.","category":"record","source":"Trail camera","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Someone was inside the marquee for seventeen minutes before six and never went near the gate, against Gwen''s claim that she stayed away until eight, and the van driver went nowhere near it.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Cloth from the marquee bin","description":"Marquee inspection: a square of blue cloth in the marquee bin, wet with boiled linseed, cut from the stewards'' roll; the roll in the box, signed out to G. Pardoe on Friday, has one square missing.","category":"physical","source":"Marquee inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The oil went on with a cloth from the box she signed for.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Message to a friend on Friday night","description":"Phone message, Friday 21:37, from Gwen: \"Second again tomorrow by the look of it. Rent is due in April and that double plot was the point of the whole year.\"","category":"testimony","source":"Phone message","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"She knew she had lost, and what losing cost her, the night before the oil went on.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Entry list, plot applications and the box","description":"Show office: W. Bray, entry nine, marrow, third favourite; applications for the double plot by the trough from R. Tapley, G. Pardoe and W. Bray; the stewards'' box, holding the only blue ties and the only cloth roll on the field, signed out to G. Pardoe at 21:05 on Friday and signed back in at the briefing at 08:06 on Saturday.","category":"record","source":"Show office","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Wilf had a marrow and a plot application of his own, and the only blue ties and cloth on the field spent the night at Gwen''s.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Plot-holder on the van at the gate","description":"Plot-holder''s statement: a van stood at the field gate with its lights on at about half past six on Saturday, and somebody walked away from it through the hedge gap towards the marquee.","category":"red_herring","source":"Plot-holder''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The van was the milk delivery for the tea tent. The trail camera has the driver leaving a crate at the gap and walking back to the van.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"How long does linseed take to show on a marrow?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"When were you last inside that marquee before the judge went in?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"What does the shield get the winner, besides the shield?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Who had the box of ties and cloths over the weekend?","category":"access","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Does Wilf go inside on his rope check?","category":"timeline","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Why does this marrow matter so much after twelve years?","category":"motive","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-7","text":"What did you take into the marquee with you?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"Who was on the field before seven?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"The flap was cut and retied with ties from the stewards'' box, so someone entitled to that box went in after nine."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The camera has a steward in the marquee before six, by the hedge gap that avoids the gate book, against her claim that she stayed away until eight, and clears the van."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The oily cloth is cut from the roll signed out to her."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"She wrote off her chances the night before and named the rent and the plot as the stake."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The van was the milk delivery, and the camera shows the driver going no further than the gap."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["How quickly does linseed show on skin like that?","Could the oil have gone on at seven?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"An hour of oil is enough for the gloss and the smell, so the oil went on well before the steward opened up."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Did you go back to the marquee before eight?","Where were you at six on Saturday?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She claims the gate at two minutes to eight was her first time on the field, then concedes the early visit when the camera is put to her."},{"questionId":"question-7","alternativePhrasings":["Where did the blue ties come from?","Who signed the cloth roll out?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The ties and the cloth were in the box signed out to her."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["Is there money in winning?","What is the double plot to you?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"The rent and the plot were the stake, and she had lost to Ron three years running."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='198b686da6121e5f1bf17c0dcf9d59555ca5c763a3dd4f077efde81d727c9bdb');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"9b89e4f7-5d64-4eb8-a022-22d4482ee3b2","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Twelve Grams Light","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Saltmarsh amateur orchestra","crime":"A patron''s antique violin bow, loaned to the orchestra''s leader for Saturday''s concert, was swapped for a trade copy before the concert began.","difficulty":"medium","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"6f612628f72150db264186eddd583067d9264b7b8022c6971036311a13c4d800","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"The Saltmarsh Orchestra plays its spring concert at St Oswin''s hall, and this year the patron lent the leader an antique bow for the slow movement. The orchestra secretary signed its case into the green room at twenty-five to six on Saturday. At five past seven, tuning up, the leader felt the weight wrong in her hand and found a plain pearl eye on the frog where the loaned bow carries a gold-ringed one. The bow in the case was a trade copy worth less than a night''s hall hire. Pia Hollowell, principal second violin, was alone in the green room with the case from twenty past six until five to seven. She rehairs bows for a living and wrote the insurance valuation for this one in March. The patron wants an answer before the insurer rings on Monday.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Pia Hollowell","ageRange":"late twenties","role":"principal second violin and bow restorer","avatarDescription":"Black concert dress with a cardigan over it, rosin dust on the cuffs, hair pinned up and coming down.","speechStyle":"Precise about instruments, vague about herself, and apt to trail off with \"anyway\".","attitude":"Pleasant and self-possessed, amused by the fuss, keeps glancing at the clock.","relationship":"Six seasons in the orchestra. Her bow workshop is behind on its rent, and she wrote the insurance valuation for the loaned bow."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"secretary","actualEvent":"The orchestra secretary collected the bow from the patron on Thursday, moved it that night into her own case and put a trade copy she had ordered on Tuesday into the patron''s case, then signed the copy into the green room on Saturday as the loan.","motive":"The secretary meant to sell the antique bow privately to cover money she had borrowed from the orchestra''s accounts.","opportunity":"She had the bow at home for two nights between the patron''s house and the hall.","means":"A trade copy of the same pattern, ordered from the supplier''s catalogue on the orchestra''s trade account, which she runs, and delivered to her address on Thursday morning.","realAlibi":"I was in the green room from twenty past six, mostly on the phone, then went out to my car for strings and came back for the tuning call.","explanation":"The leader''s photo of the open case, taken at eleven minutes to six for the orchestra''s page, already shows a plain pearl eye on the frog, so the copy was in the case half an hour before Pia walked in. The supplier''s order for the copy sits on the orchestra''s trade account, which the secretary holds for strings, rosin and youth section bows, and it was delivered to her home on the Thursday she collected the loan. Pia''s half hour alone was a call to another orchestra about a paid seat, and the bow case she carried to the car came back with a packet of strings for her stand partner.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"secretary","at":"2026-04-16T14:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-16T14:20:00Z","location":"patron''s house","description":"The secretary collects the loaned bow from the patron.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"secretary","at":"2026-04-16T21:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-16T21:30:00Z","location":"secretary''s house","description":"The secretary moves the antique bow to her own case and puts the trade copy in the patron''s case.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"secretary","at":"2026-04-18T17:30:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T17:36:00Z","location":"green room","description":"The secretary signs the patron''s case into the green room.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"leader","at":"2026-04-18T17:48:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T17:50:00Z","location":"green room","description":"The leader signs in, photographs the open case for the orchestra page and goes to warm up.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T18:20:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T18:55:00Z","location":"green room","description":"Pia sits with the second violin parts, then phones the Wyvern Sinfonia about a paid seat.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-6","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T18:56:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T19:01:00Z","location":"car park","description":"Pia fetches a packet of strings from her car.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-7","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T19:02:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T19:30:00Z","location":"stage","description":"Pia tunes with the section while the leader raises the wrong bow.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-8","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-04-18T21:40:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T21:41:00Z","location":"band room","description":"Pia sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"green room","from":"2026-04-18T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T20:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"secretary","location":"green room","from":"2026-04-18T17:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T20:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"leader","location":"green room","from":"2026-04-18T17:00:00Z","until":"2026-04-18T20:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The patron''s bow has a gold ring round the pearl eye on the frog, and a trade copy of that pattern has a plain eye.","disclosurePhrases":["The real one has a gold ring round the eye.","The patron''s bow has a ringed pearl eye.","A trade copy has a plain pearl eye.","The frog eye is how you spot the difference."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I spent most of my time in the green room on the phone to the Wyvern Sinfonia about a paid seat.","disclosurePhrases":["I was on the phone to the Wyvern.","I rang the Wyvern about a paid seat.","Most of that half hour I was on a call.","The Wyvern have offered me a paid chair."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"I buy my copies and stock from Larkspur on my own trade account, and my last order from them was in January.","disclosurePhrases":["I buy from Larkspur on my own account.","My last Larkspur order was in January.","I have my own trade account with Larkspur.","Half the county buys from Larkspur."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I went out to my car for a packet of strings for my stand partner and was back inside in four minutes.","disclosurePhrases":["I went to the car for strings.","My stand partner snapped an E string.","I was back inside in four minutes.","That case had a packet of strings in it."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"My workshop is three months behind on its rent, and I wrote the valuation for the patron''s insurer.","disclosurePhrases":["The workshop is three months behind on rent.","I wrote the valuation for the insurer.","Money has been tight at the workshop.","I valued that bow for the patron myself."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["Who swapped the bow.","What the secretary ordered or from whom.","What the leader''s photo shows.","Anything about the orchestra''s accounts."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I spent most of my time in the green room on the phone to the Wyvern Sinfonia about a paid seat.","permittedDeception":"I may say I was going through the second violin parts and marking bowings the whole time, until the detective presses twice."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Leader on the bow","description":"Leader''s statement: she looked in at the open case at about ten to six, saw nothing amiss, photographed the loaned bow for the orchestra''s page, went to warm up in the hall, and at five past seven at the tuning call the bow sat light in her hand and the frog carried a plain pearl eye.","category":"testimony","source":"Leader''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"She looked at that case before anyone else went in and took the bow in it for the patron''s. What the photo shows decides when the swap happened.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Green room sheet","description":"Green room sheet, Saturday: patron''s bow case in 17:35, signed by the secretary; leader in 17:48, out 17:50; P. Hollowell in 18:20, out 18:55; leader in 19:00; the case stayed on the side table throughout and the sheet carries no other names.","category":"record","source":"Green room sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Thirty-five minutes alone with the case, on a sheet that shows two other people in the room for two minutes each.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"The bow found in the case","description":"Bow examination: a trade copy in the same pattern, twelve grams light, plain pearl eye, stamped M. Larkspur and Sons, the trade supplier most restorers in the county buy from; the patron''s bow is nowhere in the hall.","category":"physical","source":"Bow examination","kind":"observation","interpretation":"A copy anyone in the trade could order. The stamp narrows it to half the county.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Insurance valuation letter","description":"Valuation letter, March, signed P. Hollowell for the patron''s insurer: the bow valued at a figure that would clear a small workshop''s debts twice over, with a note that the supplier''s catalogue lists a student copy of the same pattern.","category":"record","source":"Valuation letter","kind":"observation","interpretation":"She knew the price and she knew a copy existed. So did anyone the patron showed the letter to.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Photo from the leader''s phone","description":"Phone photo, Saturday 17:49, the open case on the green room side table: at full zoom the frog eye is plain pearl with no ring round it.","category":"record","source":"Phone photo","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The copy was already in the case thirty minutes before Pia signed in.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Pia''s call record","description":"Phone record: outgoing call 18:31 to 18:53 on Saturday to the office number of the Wyvern Sinfonia.","category":"record","source":"Phone record","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Twenty-two of her thirty-five minutes went on a call she tried to hide, about a job rather than a bow.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Supplier''s order record","description":"Supplier order, M. Larkspur and Sons: one student copy, pattern as the patron''s bow, ordered on the Tuesday on the Saltmarsh Orchestra''s trade account, which the secretary holds for strings, rosin and youth section bows and which ships to her home address because the orchestra has no office; delivered Thursday morning. Pia''s own trade account with the firm shows a rehair order in January and nothing since.","category":"record","source":"Supplier order","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The copy was bought on the account the secretary runs, not Pia''s, and delivered to the secretary''s door the day she collected the real bow.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Car park camera","description":"Car park camera: 18:57 Pia at her car with the boot open; 19:01 back through the stage door with a small packet in her hand and the bow case under her arm both ways.","category":"record","source":"Car park camera","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Four minutes, a packet of strings, and the case she took out came back.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Stage manager on the car park","description":"Stage manager''s statement: a few minutes before seven Pia came out of the green room with a slim bow case under her arm and went straight out to the car park, then came back in before the doors opened.","category":"red_herring","source":"Stage manager''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The car park camera has her back inside in four minutes with a packet of strings. The case was her own, and it came back with her.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"What tells the real bow from a copy?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"What were you doing in that room for half an hour with the case?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"Where do you buy your copies and stock?","category":"access","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Why did you take a case out to your car before the doors opened?","category":"evidence","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"How is the rehair business doing?","category":"motive","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"Why keep a paid seat somewhere else quiet?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"When did you last order anything from Larkspur?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"Had you handled that bow before Saturday?","category":"evidence","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The leader photographed the case before Pia went in, so the photo fixes when the swap happened."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The frog eye is already plain at eleven minutes to six, half an hour before Pia signed in."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The copy was ordered on the account the secretary runs and delivered to her door the day she collected the loan."},{"evidenceId":"record-4","why":"The trip to the car was four minutes for strings, and the case came back with her."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-4","why":"The case she carried out was her own and came back with her; the camera has strings in her hand."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["What is different about the real bow?","Could someone spot the copy from across the room?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The ringed eye marks the real bow, and a photo of the frog settles which bow was in the case."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["Do you have an account with Larkspur?","Where would a copy like that come from?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"Her own account is quiet since January, and the supplier''s records put the copy on the orchestra''s account instead."},{"questionId":"question-4","alternativePhrasings":["Why did you go out to the car park?","What was in the case you took out?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"The trip was for strings and the camera backs it."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='6f612628f72150db264186eddd583067d9264b7b8022c6971036311a13c4d800');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"51e29950-c819-4c53-a3b8-07140621ad68","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"The Cream Paper Letters","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Ashcombe village choir","crime":"Five members of the Ashcombe Singers received anonymous typed letters over three weeks, each unkind about their singing or their place in the choir, and the choir split into camps over who had written them.","difficulty":"hard","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"cdf9a551339e234727f781ee974e4136d750b454b793539e49579d7ce8c79153","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Over three weeks in February, five members of the Ashcombe Singers opened unsigned letters. Each was typed on cream laid paper, each was unkind about the reader''s voice or their place in the choir, and each landed on something said at rehearsal. By the third one the whole choir had taken sides, the altos had stopped speaking to the sopranos, and the newcomer with the spring solo had stopped coming. The paper matches the choir''s own stock in the music store. A line from the third letter, that the newcomer sings like a kettle, was said by the chair in the hall kitchen at the Thursday rehearsal before it arrived, with Corin Blythe, the accompanist, at the sink. Corin booked the hall for piano practice on the evening of the tenth, signed in at two minutes past eight and never signed out, and the postmistress saw her at the village box that night with a thick envelope. The committee turned Corin down for musical director in January. Question her, then decide.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Corin Blythe","ageRange":"mid forties","role":"choir accompanist","avatarDescription":"Navy jumper with the sleeves pushed up, a music case held flat on her knee, a wedding ring she turns while she thinks.","speechStyle":"Measured and exact, pauses before dates and numbers.","attitude":"Guarded and courteous, worn thin by the subject and says so.","relationship":"Accompanist for eleven years and keeper of the music store. Applied for musical director in January and was turned down four votes to three."},"truth":{"guilty":false,"culpritId":"alto-leader","actualEvent":"Hazel Penrose, the alto section leader, let herself into the hall with the committee door code on three evenings over four weeks, printed the letters from her tablet onto the choir''s cream paper, and posted them in pairs, having addressed the first one to herself.","motive":"She wanted the newcomer out of the spring solo and the new director blamed for a choir at war, so that the committee would reverse his appointment.","opportunity":"As a committee member she had the door code and knew the hall diary, including that Corin''s practice bookings ended early on band nights.","means":"The choir''s cream paper from the music store and the hall printer, fed from her own tablet.","realAlibi":"I was at the hall piano until about twenty to nine, then I drove over to the Fox and Hounds and played until closing.","explanation":"The hall printer logs every job with the sending device, and the five letters are five pages from a tablet named for Hazel Penrose on three evenings: two on the twenty-seventh of January, two on the tenth of February at 21:44 and 21:46, an hour after Corin had gone, and one on the seventeenth. The door keypad has the committee code entered at 21:38 on the tenth. Corin''s card paid for a round at the Fox and Hounds at 21:52, four miles away, and the landlord''s photo of the band has her at the keyboard at half past nine. The kettle line went through the open kitchen hatch to the altos queuing for tea at the Thursday rehearsal, and Hazel repeated it on the car share home that night. The thick envelope at the box was the band''s festival entry, acknowledged by email the next morning.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"alto-leader","at":"2026-01-27T19:10:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T19:20:00Z","location":"village hall","description":"Hazel prints the first two letters from her tablet after the committee meeting breaks up.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-10T20:02:00Z","until":"2026-02-10T20:40:00Z","location":"village hall","description":"Corin runs through the spring programme at the piano and leaves the door on the code.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-10T20:44:00Z","until":"2026-02-10T20:46:00Z","location":"village postbox","description":"Corin posts the band''s festival entry on her way out of the village.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-10T21:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-10T23:00:00Z","location":"Fox and Hounds","description":"Corin plays keyboard with the band and pays for a round at the bar.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"alto-leader","at":"2026-02-10T21:38:00Z","until":"2026-02-10T21:58:00Z","location":"village hall","description":"Hazel lets herself in with the door code, takes paper from the music store and prints two letters from her tablet.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-6","actorId":"alto-leader","at":"2026-02-11T08:10:00Z","until":"2026-02-11T08:12:00Z","location":"village postbox","description":"Hazel posts the two letters.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-7","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-02-21T15:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-21T15:01:00Z","location":"village hall","description":"Corin sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"village hall","from":"2026-02-10T19:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-10T23:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"alto-leader","location":"village hall","from":"2026-01-27T18:00:00Z","until":"2026-01-27T22:00:00Z"},{"actorId":"alto-leader","location":"village hall","from":"2026-02-10T19:00:00Z","until":"2026-02-10T23:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"The hall printer keeps a log of every job with the time and the name of the device that sent it.","disclosurePhrases":["The hall printer keeps a job log.","That printer logs the device name on every job.","Every print job goes into the printer log.","The printer log will have the device on it."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I left the hall at about twenty to nine and played keyboard with a folk band at the Fox and Hounds until closing.","disclosurePhrases":["I was at the Fox with the band.","I play keys with a folk band on Tuesdays.","I left the hall about twenty to nine.","The band had me at the Fox till closing."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The kitchen hatch was open and the altos were queuing at it when the chair came out with the kettle line.","disclosurePhrases":["The hatch was open when she came out with it.","Half the altos were queuing at the hatch.","The altos heard the kettle line through the hatch.","That kettle remark went straight through the hatch."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"The thick envelope I posted that night was the band''s entry form for the summer festival.","disclosurePhrases":["That envelope was the band''s festival entry.","I posted our festival form that night.","The thick envelope was a festival entry.","The band''s entry went in the box that night."],"prerequisiteFactIds":["fact-2"]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"Everyone on the committee has the hall door code, and the cream paper lives in the music store.","disclosurePhrases":["The whole committee has the door code.","The cream paper sits in the music store.","Anyone on the committee can let themselves in.","Seven people have that door code."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-6","statement":"I applied for the director''s post because it carries a stipend and the accompanist''s chair pays expenses only.","disclosurePhrases":["The director gets a stipend, the accompanist gets expenses.","I went for the job for the stipend.","The post pays and my chair pays expenses.","I wanted the stipend as much as the baton."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"doesNotKnow":["Who wrote the letters.","That anyone entered the hall after she left.","What the printer log shows.","What was said on the altos'' car share."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5","fact-6"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I left the hall at about twenty to nine and played keyboard with a folk band at the Fox and Hounds until closing.","permittedDeception":"I may say I stayed at the hall piano until ten and drove straight home, until the detective presses twice."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"The five letters","description":"Letters: five sheets of cream laid paper, the same stock as the choir''s music store, typed in one plain face; envelopes in block capitals, posted from the village box in pairs on the fourth and the eleventh and singly on the twentieth; one tells the newcomer the solo was given out of pity, one tells an alto that after twenty years in the section she still reads from the page.","category":"physical","source":"Letters","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Choir paper, choir gossip, choir postbox. Written by somebody inside, and that is as far as the paper takes it.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Chair on the kettle line","description":"Chair''s statement: the remark that the newcomer sings like a kettle was hers, made in the hall kitchen during the tea break at the Thursday rehearsal before the third letter, with Corin at the sink and nobody else in the kitchen.","category":"testimony","source":"Chair''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"Nobody else in the kitchen. The hatch to the hall is another matter.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Hall diary and printer counter","description":"Hall diary: C. Blythe, piano practice, Tuesday the tenth, 20:00 to 22:00, signed in 20:02 with no time out; the hall printer''s page counter shows two pages that evening, the only pages that week, and five pages in all since the last week of January, every one on an evening the hall was in use.","category":"record","source":"Hall diary","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Two pages printed on a night she had booked the hall, and five pages for five letters. The printer knows more than the diary does.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Committee minutes, January","description":"Committee minutes, January: the musical director post goes to the outside candidate, four votes to three, over the accompanist''s application; the spring concert solo confirmed for the newcomer, section leaders to be told in person.","category":"record","source":"Committee minutes","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Corin lost the vote and the newcomer kept the solo. Three people voted her way, and nobody at that table is a stranger to the paper.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Hall printer job log and door keypad","description":"Printer log: 27 January 19:12, \"Document\", two pages, from device \"Penrose tablet\"; 10 February 21:44 and 21:46, \"Document\", one page each, same device; 17 February 20:58, \"Document\", one page, same device; the only other job since Christmas is programme notes from Corin''s laptop on the twentieth of January. Door keypad: the committee code entered at 21:38 on the tenth, the door relocked at 21:58.","category":"record","source":"Printer log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"All five letters went to the printer from Hazel Penrose''s tablet across three evenings, and on the tenth she let herself in an hour after Corin had gone.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-1"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Fox and Hounds card payment and band photo","description":"Pub records: card payment in Corin''s name at the Fox and Hounds, four miles from Ashcombe, at 21:52 on the tenth; the landlord''s photo of the band, posted at 21:31, has her at the keyboard.","category":"record","source":"Pub records","kind":"observation","interpretation":"She was four miles away when the print jobs ran.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Newcomer on the car share","description":"Newcomer''s statement: on the car share home from that Thursday rehearsal, Hazel Penrose repeated to her, laughing, that the chair thought she sang like a kettle, a week before the letter said the same.","category":"testimony","source":"Newcomer''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The kettle line went through the hatch and out of the kitchen in an alto''s mouth.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Festival entry acknowledgement","description":"Email, Wednesday the eleventh, 09:12, from the Harrowmoor folk festival to the band''s address: entry form received by post, with Corin named on keyboard.","category":"record","source":"Email","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The thick envelope was the band''s entry, posted on her way to the pub.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Postmistress on the village box","description":"Postmistress''s statement: at about a quarter to nine on the evening of the tenth she saw Corin push a thick envelope into the village box, and two more letters were delivered that same week.","category":"red_herring","source":"Postmistress''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The thick envelope was the folk band''s festival entry. The festival acknowledged it by email on the eleventh.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"Could the letters have come off the hall printer?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Where were you by half past nine on the night you booked the hall?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"Was it just you in the kitchen when the chair made the kettle remark?","category":"evidence","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Who can get into the hall, and where does the choir keep its paper?","category":"access","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"What would the director''s job have been worth to you?","category":"motive","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-6"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"What was in the envelope the postmistress saw you post?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Who sends things to that printer?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-1"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"innocent","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-3","why":"Two pages printed on her booked night with no time out, and five pages for five letters. The printer log, once found, says who sent them and when."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"Every letter came from Hazel Penrose''s tablet, and the keypad has her entering an hour after Corin had gone."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"A card payment and a photo put Corin four miles away while the letters were printing."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"The kettle line reached the altos through the hatch, so the chair''s kitchen puts nobody at the typewriter."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-5","why":"The envelope was the band''s festival entry, acknowledged by the festival the next morning."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Does the hall printer keep any record?","Where would someone print on choir paper?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The printer log names the device that sent each job."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["When did you leave the hall?","Can anyone place you after nine?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"She claims the piano until ten, then concedes the band at the Fox when pressed."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["Who else could have heard the kettle remark?","Was the kitchen hatch open?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The altos were queuing at the hatch when it was said."},{"questionId":"question-6","alternativePhrasings":["What did you post that night?","Was that envelope the letters?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"The envelope was the band''s festival entry."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='cdf9a551339e234727f781ee974e4136d750b454b793539e49579d7ce8c79153');
select public.publish_scenario('{"scenarioId":"fe5097f9-0c42-4cc6-a006-6aa85e9f8675","schemaVersion":1,"generatorVersion":"handcrafted-2","version":1,"title":"Call Point Four","genre":"cozy procedural","setting":"Gullhaven lifeboat station museum","crime":"A manual call point in the boathouse corridor was pressed during the museum''s Sunday fundraiser, emptying the building for twenty-five minutes with no fire and no emergency, and the auction was abandoned.","difficulty":"easy","estimatedDurationSeconds":180,"fingerprint":"cde1ee71c05d23c2d9d0c79878a3c4f536fc59cacd2d5d5983cc75e800747405","status":"validated","publicBriefing":"Gullhaven''s old lifeboat station runs as a museum, and its roof fund depends on the Sunday auction. At twenty-one minutes past two, with the hall full and the auctioneer on lot eight, the sounders went and sixty people filed out onto the slipway. There was no fire. The panel shows a call point in the boathouse corridor pressed by hand, and the auction was abandoned with the star lot, a coxswain''s medal set, held over to the autumn. Ewan Kerrick, the coxswain''s son, volunteers on the winch and was rostered alone in the winch house at the foot of the slipway from two until half past. He had asked the committee twice to withdraw his father''s medals. The corridor door onto the slipway was found propped with a fender, and a visitor saw a lad in a red hoodie run out of that corridor as the alarm went. The call point cover carries a smear of grease. Ewan has asked to get this over with before the tide turns.","suspect":{"id":"suspect","name":"Ewan Kerrick","ageRange":"late fifties","role":"winch volunteer","avatarDescription":"Faded station fleece, grease in the creases of both hands, a grey beard trimmed close.","speechStyle":"Slow, drifts into station jargon and stops to find the plain word.","attitude":"Civil and unhurried, warms up when the boats come up.","relationship":"Son of the station''s last coxswain and a volunteer for twenty years. His father''s medals were the auction''s star lot and the museum''s best hope for the roof."},"truth":{"guilty":true,"culpritId":"suspect","actualEvent":"At a quarter past two Ewan dropped the winch house shutter, walked up the slipway and through the propped corridor door, pressed the call point beside that door with a greasy thumb, and was back on the slipway among the crowd before the hall had emptied.","motive":"The committee had refused twice to withdraw his father''s medals from the auction, and an abandoned sale was the only way left to keep them within the family''s reach for another season.","opportunity":"The winch house opens onto the slipway, the corridor door is propped during demonstrations, and the whole hall was watching the auctioneer.","means":"A thumb on a resettable call point, with marine grease from the winch tub still on it.","realAlibi":"I was down at the winch with a crowd of kids from two o''clock and I came up the slipway when the sounders went.","explanation":"A visitor''s photo on the slipway at 14:19 shows the winch house shutter down with the back in ten minutes card on it, and at the top of the frame a man in a station fleece going in at the propped corridor door, against his account of a crowd at the winch straight through to the alarm. The call point is beside that door, at the slipway end of the corridor. The grease on its cover is the marine grade kept in the winch house tub, and his message to the chair the night before asked for lot nine to be pulled a third time. The lad in the red hoodie was in the gents at the hall end of the corridor, in the hall camera''s frame, when the point was pressed; he ran for the slipway door because the alarm went. Ewan waited inside the door until the auctioneer called lot nine, which the tape times, so that the medals were never put up. A parent saw a station fleece go in at the door; eleven volunteers wear one, and only Ewan had opened the grease tub that day, with the winch house key on his belt. The winch sheet with no break on it is in his own hand.","timeline":[{"id":"time-1","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-17T14:00:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T14:14:00Z","location":"winch house","description":"Ewan runs the winch for a crowd of children.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-2","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-17T14:15:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T14:16:00Z","location":"winch house","description":"Ewan drops the shutter and hangs the back in ten minutes card.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-3","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-17T14:17:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T14:21:00Z","location":"boathouse corridor","description":"Ewan comes through the propped slipway door and presses the call point beside it.","requiresAccess":true},{"id":"time-4","actorId":"visitor-lad","at":"2026-05-17T14:20:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T14:21:00Z","location":"gents toilet","description":"A teenager in a red hoodie is in the gents at the hall end of the corridor when the sounders start, and runs for the slipway door.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-5","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-17T14:22:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T14:50:00Z","location":"slipway","description":"Ewan stands among the crowd on the slipway until the panel is reset.","requiresAccess":false},{"id":"time-6","actorId":"suspect","at":"2026-05-17T15:40:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T15:41:00Z","location":"crew room","description":"Ewan sits down for the interview.","requiresAccess":false}],"access":[{"actorId":"suspect","location":"boathouse corridor","from":"2026-05-17T13:00:00Z","until":"2026-05-17T17:00:00Z"}]},"knowledge":{"facts":[{"id":"fact-1","statement":"A pressed call point sets every sounder in the building off, and the panel shows which point went and when.","disclosurePhrases":["Any call point empties the whole building.","The panel shows which point was pressed.","Press one point and every sounder goes.","The panel keeps the point and the time."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-2","statement":"I dropped the winch house shutter and hung the back in ten minutes card at about a quarter past two.","disclosurePhrases":["I put the shutter down about quarter past two.","I shut the winch house for a few minutes.","The back in ten card went up at quarter past.","I closed the winch house before the alarm went."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-3","statement":"The grease on my hands that afternoon was the marine grease from the tub in the winch house.","disclosurePhrases":["My hands were covered in winch grease.","That marine grease lives in the winch house tub.","I had winch grease on me all afternoon.","The winch runs on that marine grease."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-4","statement":"I wanted my father''s medals withdrawn from the auction and messaged the chair about it again on Saturday night.","disclosurePhrases":["I messaged the chair on Saturday night about the medals.","Those are my father''s medals on that sheet.","I wanted lot nine out of the sale.","Twice I went to the committee about the medals."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]},{"id":"fact-5","statement":"The slipway door at the end of the corridor is propped with a fender during demonstrations so the winch team can reach the hall.","disclosurePhrases":["We prop the slipway door during demonstrations.","The fender keeps the slipway door open.","The winch team comes and goes by that door.","That door stays propped whenever the winch runs."],"prerequisiteFactIds":[]}],"knows":["fact-1","fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"doesNotKnow":["That a visitor photographed the winch house.","What the hall camera recorded.","Whether the grease on the cover has been tested."],"admitsImmediately":["fact-1"],"revealsWhenQuestioned":["fact-2","fact-3","fact-4","fact-5"],"misleading":[{"factId":"fact-2","realFact":"I dropped the winch house shutter and hung the back in ten minutes card at about a quarter past two.","permittedDeception":"I may say I had the winch running for the children from two straight through until the sounders sent everyone out, and that I never left the slipway, until the photo is put to me."}]},"publicEvidence":[{"id":"exhibit-1","title":"Fire panel event log","description":"Alarm panel log, Sunday: 14:21:07 manual call point CP-4, boathouse corridor, activated; no detector heads in alarm; building confirmed clear 14:25; sounders silenced 14:33; system reset 14:46. The auctioneer''s tape has lot eight knocked down at 14:20:50 and lot nine called at 14:21:05.","category":"record","source":"Alarm panel log","kind":"observation","interpretation":"Pressed by hand two seconds after lot nine was called, in a corridor off a full hall. Whoever did it was back in the crowd inside four minutes.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-3","title":"Auction sheet","description":"Auction sheet: lots one to eight sold; lot nine, coxswain''s medal set, reserve set by the committee; sale abandoned at the alarm and lot nine held to the autumn auction; note from the chair that the roof fund is now short.","category":"record","source":"Auction sheet","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The only lot left unsold is the one somebody asked twice to have withdrawn.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-4","title":"Chair on the medals and the winch sheet","description":"Chair''s statement: Ewan asked the committee twice, in March and in April, to withdraw his father''s medals from the auction, and was told each time that the roof came first; he took it quietly and kept turning up for the winch, has run the roof appeal''s collecting tins for two years, and counted Sunday''s door money himself. The winch demonstration sheet, filled in by Ewan after the reset and countersigned by the chair, has him down for two until half past two, twelve children, with no break marked.","category":"testimony","source":"Chair''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"He wanted that lot pulled and was refused, and the sale that collapsed was his own appeal''s. His sheet has him at the winch throughout.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}},{"id":"exhibit-5","title":"Boathouse corridor notes","description":"Corridor inspection: the corridor runs from the hall to the slipway; the gents toilet opens off it at the hall end, the slipway door at the far end stands propped open with a fender, and call point CP-4 is on the wall beside that door with a dark smear across its cover; the toilets were in use by visitors all afternoon. A parent on the slipway saw somebody in a station fleece go in at that door a minute or two before the alarm and took them for staff; eleven volunteers wear the fleece.","category":"physical","source":"Corridor inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"An open door from the slipway, a greasy thumb on the point beside it, a corridor the public were using at the other end, and a fleece that eleven people own.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"discoverableEvidence":[{"id":"record-1","title":"Visitor''s slipway photo","description":"Visitor''s phone photo, 14:19, taken on the slipway: the winch house shutter is down with a card reading \"back in ten minutes\" and nobody is at the winch; at the top of the frame a man in a station fleece with a grey beard is stepping in at the propped corridor door.","category":"record","source":"Visitor''s phone photo","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The winch house was shut two minutes before the call point went, against his own sheet and his account of a crowd there until the alarm, and a bearded man in a station fleece was at the door the call point sits beside, with two minutes to wait for lot nine.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-2"]}},{"id":"record-2","title":"Grease from the call point cover","description":"Corridor inspection: the smear on the CP-4 cover is the marine grease kept in the winch house tub, a grade used nowhere else in the museum; a matching thumb smear sits on the push plate of the slipway door; the winch house was locked with the key on Ewan''s belt until he opened it at two, and the chair''s rota has the other two winch volunteers on the hall door and the tea urn all afternoon.","category":"physical","source":"Corridor inspection","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The thumb that pressed the point had been in the winch tub, which only he had opened that day, and it came in through the slipway door.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-3"]}},{"id":"record-3","title":"Message to the chair on Saturday","description":"Phone message, Saturday 18:40, from Ewan to the chair: \"Pull lot nine. I would rather the roof leaked another year than watch Dad''s medals go to a stranger.\"","category":"testimony","source":"Phone message","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"A third request, the night before, with the stakes in his own words.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-4"]}},{"id":"record-4","title":"Hall camera at the corridor door","description":"Hall camera, which frames the hall door and the gents door at the near end of the corridor: 14:20:35 a lad in a red hoodie goes into the gents; 14:21:07 the sounders start; 14:21:12 he comes out of the gents and runs away from the camera down the corridor toward the slipway door.","category":"record","source":"Hall camera","kind":"observation","interpretation":"The red hoodie was inside the gents when CP-4 was pressed at the far end of the corridor. He ran because the alarm went.","availability":"discoverable","trigger":{"allFactIds":["fact-5"]}}],"redHerrings":[{"id":"exhibit-2","title":"Visitor on the red hoodie","description":"Visitor''s statement: a lad in a red hoodie came running out of the boathouse corridor the second the sounders went, ahead of everyone else, and straight down the slipway.","category":"red_herring","source":"Visitor''s statement","kind":"character_claim","interpretation":"The hall camera has him inside the gents at the hall end of the corridor when the point was pressed, and he ran because the alarm went.","availability":"public","trigger":{"allFactIds":[]}}],"suggestedQuestions":[{"id":"question-1","text":"One call point clears the whole building. Is that how it works?","category":"evidence","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-1"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-2","text":"Where were you when the sounders went?","category":"alibi","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-3","text":"How did you feel about your father''s medals going under the hammer?","category":"motive","priority":3,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-4","text":"Who props that corridor door open?","category":"access","priority":4,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-5","text":"Where does grease come from round here?","category":"evidence","priority":5,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-3"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-6","text":"What happens to lot nine now the sale is off?","category":"motive","priority":6,"prerequisiteIds":[],"revealsFactIds":["fact-4"],"spoilerSafety":"safe"},{"id":"question-7","text":"Where did you go when the shutter came down?","category":"follow_up","priority":1,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-2"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-5"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"},{"id":"question-8","text":"Did you come through the slipway door on Sunday?","category":"follow_up","priority":2,"prerequisiteIds":["fact-5"],"revealsFactIds":["fact-2"],"spoilerSafety":"contextual"}],"answerKey":{"verdict":"guilty","pivotal":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-1","why":"The panel has a hand on a call point in the corridor and nothing else in alarm, so somebody chose to empty the hall."},{"evidenceId":"record-1","why":"The shutter was down two minutes before the call point went, against his account of a crowd at the winch until the alarm, with a station fleece at the door the point sits beside."},{"evidenceId":"record-2","why":"The grease on the cover and the door plate is the winch tub''s grade, used nowhere else in the building."},{"evidenceId":"record-3","why":"He asked a third time the night before, naming the medals as worth more to him than the roof."}],"misleading":[{"evidenceId":"exhibit-2","why":"The lad in the red hoodie was inside the gents at the hall end when the point was pressed at the slipway end, and the hall camera has him there."}]},"solvabilityPath":[{"questionId":"question-1","alternativePhrasings":["Could the alarm have gone off by itself?","How does the panel know which point was pressed?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-1"],"why":"The panel records the point and the time, so the corridor call point was pressed by hand."},{"questionId":"question-2","alternativePhrasings":["Were you at the winch the whole time?","When did you last leave the winch house?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-2"],"why":"He claims the winch straight through, then concedes the shutter came down at a quarter past when the photo is put to him."},{"questionId":"question-5","alternativePhrasings":["What grease do you use on the winch?","Were your hands greasy on Sunday?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-3"],"why":"The grease on the cover is the winch tub''s marine grade."},{"questionId":"question-3","alternativePhrasings":["Did you want the medals sold?","Did you contact the chair about lot nine?"],"expectedFactIds":["fact-4"],"why":"He messaged the chair the night before asking for the lot to be pulled."}],"scoring":{"version":1,"verdict":500,"evidence":300,"facts":150,"time":50}}'::jsonb) where not exists(select 1 from public.scenarios where fingerprint='cde1ee71c05d23c2d9d0c79878a3c4f536fc59cacd2d5d5983cc75e800747405');
